-- Reproductive System & OB-GYN: Pathology Flashcards (110 flashcards)

-- Subtopic 1: Cervical Disease
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Cervical Disease','easy','flashcard','What do the letters CIN stand for, and how is CIN graded 1-3?','["CIN = Cervical Intraepithelial Neoplasia. CIN 1: dysplasia confined to the lower 1/3 of squamous epithelium (low-grade, often regresses). CIN 2: dysplasia of the lower 2/3 (intermediate). CIN 3: full-thickness dysplasia including carcinoma in situ (high-grade, no invasion through basement membrane)."]','A','CIN reflects the spectrum of pre-invasive cervical squamous disease. CIN 1 corresponds to LSIL (low-grade squamous intraepithelial lesion); CIN 2/3 to HSIL (high-grade). The Bethesda system groups CIN 1 as LSIL and CIN 2/3 as HSIL for cytological reporting.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Cervical Disease','easy','flashcard','What is a koilocyte and what infection does it signify?','["A koilocyte is a squamous cell showing perinuclear cytoplasmic clearing (halo) with nuclear enlargement, hyperchromasia, and irregular nuclear membrane. It is the cytopathic effect of HPV infection and its presence on cervical cytology indicates HPV-related change (CIN 1/LSIL)."]','A','Koilocytes are best seen in the intermediate cell layer of the squamous epithelium. The halo represents viral-induced cytoskeletal disruption. Their identification on Pap smear indicates low-risk productive HPV infection. They are NOT seen in high-grade CIN where the virus has integrated into the host genome.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Cervical Disease','easy','flashcard','What are the two oncoproteins of high-risk HPV and their targets?','["HPV E6 oncoprotein: binds and targets p53 for ubiquitin-mediated proteasomal degradation, preventing apoptosis and cell cycle arrest. HPV E7 oncoprotein: binds and inactivates the Rb (retinoblastoma) protein, releasing E2F transcription factors and driving uncontrolled cell cycle progression into S-phase."]','A','The E6/E7 oncoproteins are encoded by the early (E) genes of HPV. Integration of HPV DNA into the host genome disrupts the E2 gene (which normally suppresses E6/E7 transcription), leading to uncontrolled E6/E7 expression. This two-hit model targeting both p53 and Rb pathways is central to cervical carcinogenesis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Cervical Disease','medium','flashcard','What is the transformation zone and why is it targeted by Pap smear?','["The transformation zone (TZ) is the area of the cervix where squamous metaplasia occurs — columnar endocervical epithelium undergoes metaplastic transformation into squamous epithelium. It is located between the original and new squamocolumnar junctions. The TZ is the primary site where HPV infects immature metaplastic cells and CIN develops. Pap smears must sample this zone for adequate sensitivity."]','A','The position of the TZ changes with age and hormonal status — it is ectocervical in adolescence/reproductive years (ectropion) and moves endocervically in postmenopausal women. In postmenopausal women, an adequate Pap smear sample from an endocervical TZ can be difficult, reducing sensitivity unless an endocervical brush is used.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Cervical Disease','medium','flashcard','What is the natural history of CIN and which grade is most likely to regress spontaneously?','["CIN 1 (LSIL): ~60% regress, ~30% persist, ~10% progress to CIN 3, <1% progress to invasive carcinoma. CIN 2: ~40% regress, ~40% persist, ~20% progress. CIN 3: only 33% regress; ~10-20% progress to invasive carcinoma over 10-20 years if untreated. The higher the grade, the lower the regression rate and higher the progression risk."]','A','The long lag time between CIN 3 and invasive carcinoma (average 10–15 years) is why cervical screening programs are effective — they detect and treat pre-invasive disease before it becomes invasive. CIN 1 regression is driven by immune clearance of HPV; high-grade CIN persists due to integrated HPV unable to be cleared.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Cervical Disease','medium','flashcard','Describe the pathological features of invasive squamous cell carcinoma of the cervix.','["Invasive cervical SCC shows: (1) malignant squamous cells breaching the basement membrane into cervical stroma; (2) nests and cords of squamous cells with intercellular bridges, keratin pearls (well-differentiated), or undifferentiated sheets (poorly differentiated); (3) desmoplastic stromal reaction; (4) lymphovascular invasion in advanced cases. Spreads locally to parametrium, bladder, rectum; lymphatically to pelvic/para-aortic nodes; rarely haematogenously."]','A','Cervical carcinoma spreads primarily by direct local extension (parametrium → bladder/rectum → ureteric obstruction causing hydronephrosis and uraemia — a common cause of death). Lymphatic spread follows the iliac node chain. TNM and FIGO staging guide management.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Cervical Disease','hard','flashcard','What immunohistochemical marker is used as a surrogate for high-risk HPV infection in cervical biopsy specimens?','["p16INK4a (p16) — a CDK4/6 inhibitor that is upregulated when HPV E7 inactivates Rb, releasing E2F and causing compensatory p16 overexpression. Strong, diffuse block-positive p16 staining (nuclear and cytoplasmic) throughout the full thickness of the epithelium indicates high-risk HPV integration and CIN 2/3. Patchy or absent staining suggests low-grade or reactive change."]','A','p16 IHC is particularly useful in: (1) distinguishing CIN 2 (p16 positive) from immature squamous metaplasia (p16 negative); (2) diagnosing adenocarcinoma in situ vs reactive endocervical changes; (3) identifying HPV-associated oropharyngeal carcinoma. Ki-67 (MIB-1) co-staining with p16 in the upper epithelial layers further supports CIN diagnosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Cervical Disease','hard','flashcard','What are the HPV vaccine types covered and what is their mechanism of protection?','["Cervarix (bivalent): HPV 16, 18. Gardasil 4 (quadrivalent): HPV 6, 11, 16, 18. Gardasil 9 (nonavalent): HPV 6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, 58. All vaccines contain virus-like particles (VLPs) made from the L1 capsid protein of each HPV type. VLPs induce neutralising IgG antibodies that prevent initial HPV infection. They do NOT treat existing HPV infection and have no therapeutic effect on established CIN."]','A','The nonavalent vaccine (Gardasil 9) covers HPV types responsible for ~90% of cervical cancers. Vaccines are most effective when given before sexual debut (ages 9–14 ideally). Protection is prophylactic only — vaccines prevent HPV infection and CIN development but cannot clear established infection. Screening continues in vaccinated women.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Cervical Disease','easy','flashcard','What is the difference between ectocervical and endocervical epithelium?','["Ectocervix: covered by non-keratinising stratified squamous epithelium (same as vagina). Endocervix: lined by columnar mucus-secreting epithelium (single layer of tall columnar cells with basal nuclei). The squamocolumnar junction (SCJ) marks the transition between these two epithelial types. The SCJ moves with hormonal changes — ectopion (ectropion) occurs when columnar epithelium appears on the ectocervix."]','A','Cervical ectropion (previously called cervical erosion) is a physiological finding where the columnar epithelium of the endocervix is visible on the ectocervix — it is NOT a true erosion. It is common in adolescents, oral contraceptive users, and pregnancy. It may cause increased mucus discharge and contact bleeding but is benign.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Cervical Disease','medium','flashcard','What is adenocarcinoma in situ (AIS) of the cervix and how does it relate to invasive cervical adenocarcinoma?','["AIS is a glandular precursor lesion of the endocervix characterised by endocervical glands lined by malignant-appearing columnar cells (nuclear stratification, loss of cytoplasmic mucin, mitoses, apoptotic bodies) without stromal invasion. It is caused by high-risk HPV (especially HPV 18). AIS precedes invasive endocervical adenocarcinoma; treatment is excision (LEEP/cone biopsy) with clear margins."]','A','AIS is increasingly recognised due to improved HPV testing and co-testing programs. It may be multifocal and skip lesions make margin assessment critical. If margins are positive after conisation, hysterectomy is recommended, especially if fertility is not desired. p16 IHC and HPV testing aid diagnosis.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');

-- Subtopic 2: Endometrial Disease
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Endometrial Disease','easy','flashcard','What are the two types of endometrial carcinoma and how do they differ?','["Type I (Endometrioid): oestrogen-dependent; arises in peri/post-menopausal obese women via endometrial hyperplasia; well-differentiated; PTEN, KRAS, microsatellite instability mutations; good prognosis (80% 5-year survival). Type II (Non-endometrioid, mainly serous): oestrogen-independent; arises in atrophic endometrium in thin, older women; high-grade papillary serous or clear cell histology; TP53 mutations; poor prognosis (40% 5-year survival)."]','A','The dualistic model of endometrial carcinogenesis is clinically important: Type I (80% of cases) has a good prognosis and is caught early (postmenopausal bleeding). Type II is clinically silent until advanced. Uterine serous carcinoma mimics ovarian high-grade serous carcinoma histologically and molecularly (The Cancer Genome Atlas unified classification now stratifies by molecular subtype).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Endometrial Disease','easy','flashcard','List the risk factors for Type I endometrial carcinoma.','["Risk factors (all related to unopposed oestrogen): Obesity (peripheral aromatisation of androgens to oestrogen in adipose tissue); Nulliparity/infertility; Late menopause; Diabetes mellitus; Oestrogen-only HRT (without progesterone); Tamoxifen use; Polycystic ovarian syndrome (chronic anovulation); Oestrogen-secreting tumours (granulosa cell tumour); Lynch syndrome (genetic risk — mismatch repair deficiency)."]','A','Progesterone is protective against endometrial carcinoma — it induces secretory differentiation and opposes oestrogen-driven proliferation. Combined HRT (oestrogen + progestogen) does NOT increase endometrial cancer risk. The Mirena IUS (levonorgestrel-releasing) provides endometrial protection and is used therapeutically in hyperplasia management.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Endometrial Disease','easy','flashcard','What are the histological subtypes of endometrial hyperplasia and their cancer risk?','["(1) Simple hyperplasia without atypia: cystic glandular dilation, mild crowding, no cytological atypia — <1% risk of carcinoma. (2) Complex hyperplasia without atypia: complex glandular architecture (back-to-back), crowding, budding, no atypia — ~3% risk. (3) Atypical hyperplasia (simple or complex with atypia): cytological atypia (enlarged nuclei, prominent nucleoli, loss of polarity) — ~25-30% risk of progression; ~40% have concurrent carcinoma on hysterectomy."]','A','The 2014 WHO classification simplified this to two categories: hyperplasia without atypia (low risk — treat with progestins) and endometrial intraepithelial neoplasia/atypical hyperplasia (high risk — hysterectomy recommended). Atypical hyperplasia in a woman desiring fertility can be managed with high-dose progestins with close endometrial surveillance.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Endometrial Disease','medium','flashcard','How does PTEN loss contribute to endometrial carcinoma development?','["PTEN (phosphatase and tensin homolog) is a tumour suppressor that dephosphorylates PIP3, normally inhibiting the PI3K/AKT/mTOR survival pathway. Loss of PTEN (mutation, deletion, or epigenetic silencing by promoter methylation) in endometrial glands leads to constitutive PI3K/AKT/mTOR activation, promoting cell survival, proliferation, and inhibition of apoptosis. PTEN mutation is the earliest molecular event in Type I endometrial carcinogenesis, detectable even in normal-appearing endometrium adjacent to carcinoma."]','A','PTEN-null endometrial glands surrounded by normal stroma (PTEN-null glands) are considered the earliest recognisable precursor. PTEN IHC (loss of expression) is used diagnostically. Dual PI3K/AKT/mTOR pathway targeting is under investigation therapeutically. PTEN mutation coexists with microsatellite instability (MLH1 methylation) in many Type I tumours.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Endometrial Disease','medium','flashcard','What is carcinosarcoma (MMMT) of the uterus and what is its prognosis?','["Malignant mixed Mullerian tumour (MMMT) / carcinosarcoma is a highly aggressive biphasic uterine malignancy containing both malignant epithelial (carcinomatous) and malignant mesenchymal (sarcomatous) components. The sarcomatous component may be homologous (smooth muscle, fibrosarcoma) or heterologous (cartilage, striated muscle, osteoid). It is now classified as a metaplastic carcinoma (epithelial origin with sarcomatous differentiation). Prognosis is very poor — median survival <2 years."]','A','Carcinosarcomas are thought to arise from a common clonal epithelial progenitor that undergoes sarcomatous metaplasia, explaining why they behave more like high-grade carcinomas than sarcomas in terms of treatment response. They are associated with prior pelvic irradiation. Staging is surgical; chemotherapy regimens target the carcinomatous component.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Endometrial Disease','medium','flashcard','What is the significance of Lynch syndrome for gynaecological malignancies?','["Lynch syndrome (hereditary non-polyposis colorectal cancer, HNPCC) is caused by germline mutations in DNA mismatch repair genes (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2). Women with Lynch syndrome have a 40-60% lifetime risk of endometrial carcinoma (equal to or exceeding the colorectal cancer risk) and 10-12% risk of ovarian carcinoma. Endometrial cancer is often the sentinel cancer in Lynch families. Annual endometrial sampling and consideration of prophylactic hysterectomy/BSO after childbearing is recommended."]','A','Lynch-associated endometrial carcinomas tend to be younger-onset (premenopausal), lower-grade endometrioid type, and located in the lower uterine segment. IHC for MMR proteins (MLH1, MSH2, MSH6, PMS2) on tumour tissue is the screening tool, followed by germline testing if abnormal. MSI-H tumours respond to immune checkpoint inhibitors (pembrolizumab).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Endometrial Disease','hard','flashcard','How is endometrial carcinoma staged surgically (FIGO) and what is the significance of myometrial invasion depth?','["FIGO 2023 surgical staging: Stage I: confined to uterus (IA = <50% myometrial invasion; IB = ≥50%; IC = serosal involvement). Stage II: cervical stroma invasion. Stage III: regional extension (pelvic/para-aortic nodes, adnexa, vagina). Stage IV: distant metastasis (bladder, bowel mucosa, distant organs). Myometrial invasion depth >50% (Stage IB) significantly increases lymph node metastasis risk and reduces 5-year survival from ~95% (IA) to ~75% (IB)."]','A','Deep myometrial invasion is associated with lymphovascular space invasion (LVSI) and lymph node metastasis. Peritoneal cytology, previously staging, is now a prognostic factor only. Adjuvant treatment (vault brachytherapy, external beam radiotherapy, or chemotherapy) is guided by stage, grade, histological subtype, and molecular classification (PORTEC-3 trial criteria).','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Endometrial Disease','hard','flashcard','What are the four molecular subgroups of endometrial carcinoma per The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA)?','["(1) POLE ultramutated (proofreading exonuclease mutations): highest mutation burden, paradoxically excellent prognosis. (2) Microsatellite instability-high (MSI-H)/hypermutated: Lynch or MLH1 hypermethylation, intermediate prognosis. (3) Copy number low/no specific molecular profile (NSMP): KRAS/PTEN/CTNNB1 mutations, intermediate prognosis. (4) Copy number high/serous-like (TP53 mutated): TP53 somatic mutation, worst prognosis, includes serous and high-grade endometrioid carcinomas."]','A','TCGA molecular classification (now incorporated into FIGO 2023 staging as molecular qualifiers) improves prognostication beyond histology and grade alone. POLE-ultramutated tumours have excellent prognosis even if high-grade. This classification guides adjuvant therapy decisions: MSI-H tumours benefit from immune checkpoint inhibitors; TP53-mutated tumours are considered for platinum-based chemotherapy.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Endometrial Disease','easy','flashcard','What is the most common presenting symptom of endometrial carcinoma and why does this lead to early detection?','["Postmenopausal vaginal bleeding (PMB) is the cardinal symptom, occurring in ~90% of cases. Any PMB in a woman >1 year post-menopause requires urgent endometrial sampling to exclude carcinoma. Because this symptom occurs early (even Stage I disease bleeds), endometrial carcinoma is detected at an early stage in most cases — ~75% are Stage I at presentation, giving an overall 5-year survival of ~80%."]','A','In contrast to cervical and ovarian cancers, endometrial carcinoma often presents early. Premenopausal women with irregular heavy bleeding and risk factors (obesity, PCOS, tamoxifen) should also have endometrial sampling. Transvaginal ultrasound measuring endometrial thickness (>4-5 mm in postmenopausal women) is the initial investigation; Pipelle endometrial biopsy has ~90% sensitivity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Endometrial Disease','medium','flashcard','Compare the molecular features of Type I versus Type II endometrial carcinoma.','["Type I (endometrioid): PTEN loss (80%), KRAS mutations (30%), microsatellite instability (MSI, 20%), CTNNB1 (beta-catenin) mutations (40%), PIK3CA mutations (60%). All related to PI3K/AKT/MMR pathways. Type II (serous/clear cell): TP53 mutations (>90%), HER2 amplification (30-40%), CCNE1 amplification, FBXW7 mutations. Similar molecular landscape to high-grade serous ovarian carcinoma."]','A','The molecular divergence between Type I and II reflects their different pathogenetic pathways. This has therapeutic implications: PIK3CA mutations in Type I suggest PI3K inhibitor trials; HER2 amplification in Type II supports trastuzumab-based therapy (STATEC trial). TCGA classification has largely superseded the morphological Type I/II dualistic model.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');

-- Subtopic 3: Ovarian Tumours
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Ovarian Tumours','easy','flashcard','What are the three major categories of ovarian tumours and their approximate frequencies?','["(1) Surface epithelial tumours (coelomic epithelium origin): 65-70% of all ovarian tumours, 90% of ovarian malignancies. Subtypes: serous, mucinous, endometrioid, clear cell, transitional (Brenner). (2) Germ cell tumours: 15-20% of ovarian tumours, most common in young women/girls. Most are benign (mature teratoma). (3) Sex cord-stromal tumours: 5-10%. Arise from granulosa, theca, Sertoli, Leydig cells. Often hormonally active."]','A','A fourth category is metastatic tumours (5%) — Krukenberg tumours from stomach/colon. In clinical practice, age is a powerful predictor of tumour type: germ cell tumours predominate in women <25; epithelial tumours in women >40. In postmenopausal women, any new ovarian mass requires exclusion of malignancy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Ovarian Tumours','easy','flashcard','What is the clinical triad of PCOS (Rotterdam criteria)?','["Rotterdam criteria (diagnosis requires 2 of 3): (1) Oligo/anovulation (irregular or absent menstrual cycles, LH:FSH ratio >2:1). (2) Clinical or biochemical hyperandrogenism (hirsutism, acne, elevated testosterone, DHEAS). (3) Polycystic ovarian morphology on ultrasound (≥12 follicles 2-9 mm per ovary or ovarian volume >10 mL). Ovarian pathology: bilaterally enlarged ovaries with thickened cortex, multiple subcortical follicular cysts, and stromal hyperthecosis."]','A','PCOS pathogenesis involves insulin resistance → hyperinsulinaemia → stimulates ovarian theca cells (via IGF-1 receptors) to produce excess androgens → androgen excess inhibits follicular maturation → chronic anovulation. Long-term risks include endometrial hyperplasia/carcinoma (unopposed oestrogen), metabolic syndrome, T2DM, and cardiovascular disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Ovarian Tumours','easy','flashcard','What are Call-Exner bodies and in which tumour are they found?','["Call-Exner bodies are small eosinophilic hyaline droplets or spaces surrounded by granulosa cells, resembling primordial follicles. They are the pathognomonic histological feature of granulosa cell tumour (GCT) of the ovary. GCT is a sex cord-stromal tumour that secretes oestrogen, causing endometrial hyperplasia, precocious puberty (in juveniles), or postmenopausal bleeding. Adult-type GCT expresses inhibin and SF-1 on IHC."]','A','Granulosa cell tumour is the most common malignant sex cord-stromal tumour. Adult type (95%) peaks at menopause; juvenile type (5%) occurs in girls <30. Serum inhibin B and anti-Mullerian hormone (AMH) are tumour markers. Late recurrence (>5 years, even decades) is characteristic. Mutation in FOXL2 (p.C134W) is found in >95% of adult-type GCTs.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Ovarian Tumours','medium','flashcard','Compare serous versus mucinous ovarian tumours histologically and clinically.','["Serous tumours: Lined by ciliated tubal-type epithelium; bilateral in 10-20% (more common to be bilateral than mucinous); psammoma bodies present; range from cystadenoma to borderline to cystadenocarcinoma. Most common subtype of ovarian carcinoma. Mucinous tumours: Lined by tall mucin-secreting columnar cells (endocervical or intestinal type with goblet cells); typically large, multilocular, unilateral; psammoma bodies absent; CA-19-9 may be elevated in addition to CA-125. Rupture can cause pseudomyxoma peritonei."]','A','The distinction matters clinically: mucinous tumours are more likely unilateral and large (sometimes massive — cases of >100 kg reported). Primary ovarian mucinous carcinomas are rare — most apparent ovarian mucinous tumours are metastatic from GI tract. Serous carcinomas are the most lethal ovarian malignancy due to advanced stage at presentation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Ovarian Tumours','medium','flashcard','What are the tumour markers for common ovarian tumours?','["CA-125: serous carcinoma (elevated in 80% of advanced serous ovarian cancer; less sensitive for early/mucinous). AFP (alpha-fetoprotein): yolk sac tumour (endodermal sinus tumour). Beta-hCG: choriocarcinoma of the ovary. LDH: dysgerminoma (+ beta-hCG in 10%). Inhibin and AMH: granulosa cell tumour. CEA and CA-19-9: mucinous carcinoma. HE4: used with CA-125 in ROMA (risk of ovarian malignancy algorithm) to improve specificity."]','A','Tumour markers are not diagnostic alone — they are used in conjunction with imaging and clinical assessment. CA-125 can be elevated in many benign conditions (endometriosis, PID, pregnancy, fibroids). AFP is invaluable for diagnosing and monitoring yolk sac tumours in young women. All germ cell tumour markers should be checked preoperatively in young women with ovarian masses.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Ovarian Tumours','medium','flashcard','What is dysgerminoma and how is it treated?','["Dysgerminoma is the most common malignant ovarian germ cell tumour, equivalent to testicular seminoma. Peak age 10-30. Macroscopically solid, fleshy, bosselated. Microscopically: sheets of large uniform cells with clear glycogen-rich cytoplasm, central nuclei, prominent nucleoli, separated by fibrous septa with T-lymphocyte infiltrate. Markers: LDH elevated; beta-hCG in 10%; AFP negative. Radiosensitive and chemosensitive. Unilateral in 85%, so fertility-sparing surgery + BEP chemotherapy gives >95% cure rate."]','A','Dysgerminoma is isochromosome 12p positive (like all GCTs). C-KIT mutations in ~25-30% (potentially targetable). It is the only malignant GCT that is bilateral in 15% of cases — the contralateral ovary must be inspected intraoperatively. When confined to one ovary (Stage I), fertility-sparing surgery alone gives >95% cure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Ovarian Tumours','hard','flashcard','What is the two-pathway model of ovarian serous carcinogenesis?','["Type I (low-grade serous carcinoma, LGSC): develops stepwise from borderline serous tumour; BRAF or KRAS mutations; microscopically uniform cells with low mitotic rate; indolent but chemoresistant; better prognosis. Type II (high-grade serous carcinoma, HGSC): arises de novo, no identifiable precursor on ovarian surface; TP53 mutations (>96%), genomic instability; BRCA1/2 deficiency; most now thought to originate from fimbriated end of fallopian tube (serous tubal intraepithelial carcinoma — STIC); rapidly progressive; chemosensitive initially."]','A','The recognition that most HGSC arises from the fallopian tube (STIC) rather than the ovarian surface has led to the concept of risk-reducing salpingo-oophorectomy (RRSO) in BRCA mutation carriers and has changed prophylactic surgery protocols. Opportunistic salpingectomy at the time of benign gynaecological surgery is advocated for general population ovarian cancer prevention.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Ovarian Tumours','hard','flashcard','What are the Meigs syndrome features and which ovarian tumour is associated?','["Meigs syndrome is the triad of: (1) Benign ovarian fibroma (or fibrothecoma); (2) Ascites; (3) Pleural effusion (typically right-sided). The effusion and ascites resolve after tumour removal. The mechanism is thought to involve leakage of fluid through the fibroma''s surface or via diaphragmatic lymphatics. Meigs syndrome must be distinguished from malignant causes of ascites and pleural effusion — CA-125 may be mildly elevated, causing diagnostic confusion."]','A','Ovarian fibroma is a benign sex cord-stromal tumour composed of bland spindle-shaped fibroblasts producing collagen. It is solid, hard, and grey-white on cut surface. Association with Gorlin syndrome (basal cell naevus syndrome — PTCH1 mutation): bilateral fibromas in young women with calcification. Thecoma (contains theca cells) is the hormone-active variant secreting oestrogen.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Ovarian Tumours','easy','flashcard','What is a follicular cyst of the ovary and when is it clinically significant?','["A follicular cyst forms when a Graafian follicle fails to ovulate or rupture, persisting and enlarging (>3 cm). It is lined by granulosa cells, fluid-filled, thin-walled, and unilateral. Most are asymptomatic and resolve spontaneously within 4-8 weeks. They are common, non-neoplastic, functional cysts. They become clinically significant if: >5-6 cm (risk of torsion); if they rupture causing haemoperitoneum; or if they persist beyond 3 months (re-evaluate with ultrasound)."]','A','Follicular cysts are the most common cause of ovarian enlargement in reproductive-age women. Theca-lutein cysts arise from excessive hCG stimulation of multiple follicles (molar pregnancy, ovarian stimulation for IVF). Corpus luteum cysts form from a persistent corpus luteum and may cause delayed menstruation (mimicking ectopic pregnancy clinically).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Ovarian Tumours','medium','flashcard','What is a Brenner tumour and what is its characteristic histological feature?','["Brenner tumour is a benign (usually) surface epithelial ovarian tumour composed of nests of transitional (urothelial-like) epithelium embedded in a dense fibrous stroma. Characteristic: coffee-bean nuclei with longitudinal grooves (similar to urothelial cells). Usually unilateral, small, solid, and incidentally discovered. Rarely malignant. May be associated with mucinous cystadenoma in the same ovary."]','A','Brenner tumours stain positive for uroplakin, p63, and CK7, reflecting their transitional epithelial differentiation. Their fibrous stroma may produce oestrogen (from thecomatous component), causing endometrial hyperplasia. Malignant Brenner tumours are rare and must be distinguished from high-grade transitional cell carcinoma (now reclassified as high-grade serous carcinoma with transitional-like pattern).','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');

-- Subtopic 4: Uterine Pathology
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Uterine Pathology','easy','flashcard','What are the clinical features and pathological subtypes of uterine leiomyomas (fibroids)?','["Leiomyomas are benign smooth muscle tumours of the uterus — the most common uterine tumour (present in up to 70% of women by age 50). Symptoms: menorrhagia, dysmenorrhoea, pelvic pressure, urinary symptoms, infertility. Locations: intramural (within myometrium — most common), subserosal (projecting outward), submucosal (into endometrial cavity — most likely to cause bleeding and infertility), pedunculated, cervical, broad ligament. Histology: whorled fascicles of bland smooth muscle with cigar-shaped nuclei, rare mitoses, no necrosis."]','A','Fibroids are the most common indication for hysterectomy in women. They are oestrogen and progesterone receptor-positive and regress after menopause. MED12 mutations occur in ~70% of fibroids. GnRH agonists shrink fibroids by inducing medical menopause but regrowth occurs after discontinuation. Uterine artery embolisation and myomectomy are fertility-preserving options.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Uterine Pathology','easy','flashcard','Define adenomyosis and describe its pathological features.','["Adenomyosis: presence of endometrial glands and stroma within the myometrium, >2.5 mm from the basalis layer (or >1 low-power field from the endometrium-myometrium junction). Gross: diffusely enlarged, globular uterus (bulky, heavy — up to 200g vs normal 60-80g); trabeculated cut surface with haemorrhagic foci. Histology: islands of endometrial glands and stroma surrounded by hypertrophied smooth muscle. MRI: thickened junctional zone >12 mm is diagnostic. Causes dysmenorrhoea and menorrhagia in reproductive-age women; regression after menopause."]','A','Adenomyosis co-exists with other oestrogen-dependent conditions (fibroids 50%, endometriosis 15%). The pathogenesis involves downgrowth of basal endometrium into the myometrium (invagination theory). Unlike endometriosis, adenomyosis does not show cyclical bleeding at ectopic sites — the myometrial glands are not fully functional. Hysterectomy is curative; levonorgestrel IUS reduces symptoms conservatively.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Uterine Pathology','medium','flashcard','What are the pathological features of endometriosis and its common sites?','["Endometriosis: ectopic endometrial glands AND stroma outside the uterus. Sites (in order of frequency): ovaries (endometriomas/chocolate cysts — ~60%), pouch of Douglas/uterosacral ligaments, peritoneum (powder-burn/blue-black lesions), fallopian tubes, bladder/ureter, bowel, umbilicus, surgical scars. Histology: endometrial glands, stroma, and haemosiderin-laden macrophages (siderophages). Cyclical bleeding causes chronic inflammation, scarring, and adhesion formation."]','A','Three theories of pathogenesis: (1) Sampson — retrograde menstruation (best supported); (2) coelomic metaplasia — peritoneal mesothelium differentiates into endometrium; (3) lymphovascular dissemination — explains distant sites (lung, kidney). Endometriosis classifies as stage I-IV (rASRM scoring). Medical management: OCP/GnRH analogues/progestins; surgical: laparoscopic excision/ablation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Uterine Pathology','medium','flashcard','How are leiomyoma and leiomyosarcoma distinguished histologically?','["Leiomyoma (benign): rare mitoses (<4/10 HPF), no significant cytological atypia, no coagulative tumour cell necrosis, well-circumscribed, whorled. Leiomyosarcoma (malignant): requires 2 of 3 criteria — (1) ≥10 mitoses/10 HPF; (2) moderate-severe cytological atypia; (3) coagulative tumour cell necrosis (geographic necrosis of viable tumour cells — distinct from hyaline/hydropic degeneration). LMS is infiltrative, grey-yellow with necrosis; 5-year survival <50%."]','A','The distinction is critical because routine myomectomy (without suspicion of LMS) may result in uterine morcellation, disseminating LMS intraperitoneally. FDA warns against power morcellation in women with unsuspected uterine LMS. Preoperative MRI features suggesting LMS: irregular margins, necrosis, high signal on T2. STUMP (smooth muscle tumour of uncertain malignant potential) is the intermediate category.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Uterine Pathology','medium','flashcard','What are the symptoms of endometriosis and how does the extent of disease correlate with symptom severity?','["Symptoms: dysmenorrhoea (characteristically secondary — worsening over time), deep dyspareunia (particularly posterior fornix tenderness — involvement of uterosacral ligaments/pouch of Douglas), chronic pelvic pain, dyschezia (painful defaecation — rectovaginal nodules), dysuria (bladder endometriosis), infertility. Paradox: symptom severity does NOT correlate with disease extent — minimal-mild disease (Stage I-II) can cause severe pain, while Stage IV (extensive adhesions/endometrioma) may be incidentally found in asymptomatic women."]','A','Pain in endometriosis is driven by prostaglandin release, nerve fibre invasion into ectopic lesions, and central sensitisation, not purely by disease volume. This explains why ablating minimal lesions can achieve significant pain relief. Medical treatments suppress lesion activity but do not eradicate disease; definitive surgery aims to excise all endometriosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Uterine Pathology','hard','flashcard','What are the degenerative changes in leiomyomas and which is associated with pregnancy?','["Types of degeneration: (1) Hyaline degeneration — most common (65%); replacement by collagen, white glassy appearance. (2) Cystic degeneration — liquefaction of hyaline areas; large cystic spaces. (3) Calcific degeneration — calcium deposition (wombstone); seen in postmenopausal women on plain X-ray. (4) Red (carneous) degeneration — haemorrhagic infarction during pregnancy; acute pain, fever, uterine tenderness; occurs when rapidly growing fibroid outstrips blood supply. (5) Myxoid degeneration — mucoid ground substance accumulation. (6) Fatty degeneration — rare lipomatous change."]','A','Red degeneration occurs in 5-10% of fibroids during pregnancy, typically at 14-22 weeks, when the fibroid grows rapidly under hormonal influence. Management is conservative (analgesia, hydration) as surgery increases miscarriage risk. The clinical picture (localised uterine tenderness over fibroid, mild fever, elevated WBC) can mimic appendicitis, placental abruption, or preterm labour.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Uterine Pathology','hard','flashcard','Describe low-grade endometrial stromal sarcoma (LG-ESS) and its pathological features.','["LG-ESS arises from endometrial stroma and infiltrates the myometrium as worm-like plugs in lymphovascular spaces (distinctive gross appearance). Microscopically: uniform cells resembling proliferative-phase endometrial stroma with small bland nuclei, surrounding arteriole-like vessels. Mitotic count low (<5/10 HPF), mild atypia. Characterised by JAZF1-SUZ12 fusion (most common), YWHAE-NUTM2 translocation. CD10 positive, ER/PR positive. Indolent course; late recurrence (decades) possible. Treated with progestins/aromatase inhibitors for advanced/recurrent disease."]','A','The worm-like extension into myometrium (resembling uterine leiomyomatosis) and lymphovascular plugs are characteristic gross features. LG-ESS is highly ER/PR positive, explaining its responsiveness to hormonal therapy. It must be distinguished from high-grade ESS (YWHAE-NUTM2, aggressive, CD10 negative, ER/PR negative) and undifferentiated uterine sarcoma (completely undifferentiated, worst prognosis).','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Uterine Pathology','easy','flashcard','What is an endometrial polyp and what is its malignant potential?','["Endometrial polyp: focal overgrowth of endometrial glands and stroma projecting into the uterine cavity; lined by endometrial epithelium; has a fibrous stroma with thick-walled vessels distinguishing it from surrounding endometrium. Usually benign; hyperplastic or inactive glands that do not respond normally to hormonal cycling. Causes abnormal uterine bleeding (intermenstrual or postmenopausal). Malignant transformation in <1% of cases (higher risk in postmenopausal women and tamoxifen users — up to 5%)."]','A','Endometrial polyps are detected by transvaginal ultrasound as focal echogenic endometrial lesions, confirmed by saline infusion sonography (SIS) or hysteroscopy. Hysteroscopic polypectomy is both diagnostic and therapeutic. Tamoxifen-associated polyps have higher rates of atypia and carcinoma — mandatory histological assessment after removal.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Uterine Pathology','medium','flashcard','What genetic mutation is most commonly found in uterine leiomyomas and what is its significance?','["MED12 mutations (in exon 2): found in ~70% of uterine leiomyomas. MED12 encodes Mediator complex subunit 12, which regulates transcription of genes involved in cell growth. MED12-mutant fibroids have distinct morphology and molecular features. Other genetic subtypes: FH (fumarate hydratase) gene mutations — hereditary leiomyomatosis and renal cell carcinoma (HLRCC) syndrome; HMGA2 rearrangements. HLRCC-associated fibroids are multiple, numerous, develop at a young age, and are associated with cutaneous leiomyomas and aggressive renal cell carcinoma."]','A','HLRCC (hereditary leiomyomatosis and renal cell carcinoma) is an autosomal dominant syndrome caused by germline FH mutation. Women present with early-onset, symptomatic multiple fibroids (often requiring surgery before age 30). Renal cell carcinoma is papillary type 2 and highly aggressive. FH IHC (loss of expression) or germline testing identifies affected individuals for surveillance.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');

-- Subtopic 5: Gestational Trophoblastic Disease
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Gestational Trophoblastic Disease','easy','flashcard','Compare complete versus partial hydatidiform mole.','["Complete mole: karyotype 46,XX (androgenetic diploid — empty ovum fertilised by one sperm that duplicates, or by two sperm); NO fetal/embryonic tissue; ALL villi show hydropic change and circumferential trophoblastic hyperplasia; hCG markedly elevated; risk of malignant GTN 15-20%; p57KIP2 immunostain negative (no maternal DNA). Partial mole: karyotype 69,XXX or 69,XXY (triploid — normal ovum + two sperm); fetal/embryonic tissue PRESENT; focal villous hydrops; focal trophoblastic hyperplasia; milder hCG elevation; malignant GTN risk 1-5%; p57KIP2 positive."]','A','The p57KIP2 (CDKN1C) IHC stain is extremely useful in distinguishing complete (negative — no maternal DNA expression) from partial moles (positive — maternal X chromosome expressed). It also distinguishes complete mole from hydropic abortion (p57 positive in both villous cytotrophoblasts and stromal cells). This test is performed on all products of conception showing hydrops.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Gestational Trophoblastic Disease','easy','flashcard','What is the histological appearance of choriocarcinoma?','["Choriocarcinoma: sheets of anaplastic cytotrophoblasts (polygonal cells with clear cytoplasm and vesicular nuclei) and syncytiotrophoblasts (multinucleated giant cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm), WITHOUT chorionic villi. Extensive haemorrhage and necrosis. Highly invasive — destroys adjacent structures. The absence of villi distinguishes choriocarcinoma from moles (both complete and partial) and invasive mole (which retains villi). hCG is massively elevated (produced by syncytiotrophoblasts)."]','A','Gestational choriocarcinoma (arising from a prior pregnancy — mole, normal, or ectopic) is one of the most chemosensitive solid tumours known. Even with widespread metastases (lungs, brain, liver, kidney), cure rates exceed 90% with EMA-CO chemotherapy. Serum beta-hCG is a perfect surrogate for tumour burden and treatment response.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Gestational Trophoblastic Disease','easy','flashcard','What is the role of hCG monitoring after molar evacuation?','["Post-molar hCG surveillance: weekly serum beta-hCG measurements after uterine evacuation until hCG reaches undetectable levels (<5 IU/L). Normal: hCG should normalise by 8 weeks (partial mole) or 16 weeks (complete mole). Malignant GTN is diagnosed if: hCG plateau (±10%) for 3 consecutive weekly values over 3 weeks; hCG rise (>10%) over 3 consecutive values in 2 weeks; hCG remains elevated at 6 months; or histological evidence of choriocarcinoma/invasive mole. Contraception (OCP preferred) maintained during surveillance period."]','A','hCG is the ideal tumour marker for GTN: it is produced exclusively by trophoblastic tissue, has a short half-life (24-36 hours), and can detect disease at microscopic levels (1 mL of choriocarcinoma tissue produces measurable hCG). The OCP is used during surveillance to prevent a new pregnancy (which would confound hCG monitoring) and reduces false-positive hCG elevation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Gestational Trophoblastic Disease','medium','flashcard','What are the FIGO risk scoring criteria for gestational trophoblastic neoplasia?','["FIGO prognostic scoring (0-4 for each): Age (<40=0, ≥40=1); antecedent pregnancy (mole=0, abortion=1, term=2); interval from index pregnancy <4 months=0, 4-6=1, 7-12=2, >12=4; pretreatment hCG <10^3=0, 10^3-10^4=1, 10^4-10^5=2, >10^5=4; largest tumour (no tumour=0, 3-4cm=1, ≥5cm=2); site of metastases; number of metastases; prior chemotherapy. Low-risk GTN: score ≤6 (single-agent methotrexate or actinomycin-D). High-risk GTN: score ≥7 (EMA-CO combination chemotherapy)."]','A','The FIGO scoring system predicts risk of single-agent chemotherapy resistance. Low-risk GTN (score ≤6) has near-100% cure rate with single-agent therapy. High-risk GTN (score ≥7), including ultra-high risk (score >12), requires intensified EMA-CO or EMA-EP protocols. Even high-risk metastatic choriocarcinoma has >90% cure rate with appropriate chemotherapy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Gestational Trophoblastic Disease','medium','flashcard','Describe invasive mole and how it differs from choriocarcinoma.','["Invasive mole (chorioadenoma destruens): molar villi with trophoblastic hyperplasia that INVADE the myometrium or its vessels. KEY DISTINCTION from choriocarcinoma: invasive mole RETAINS identifiable chorionic villi (may appear in pulmonary/vaginal emboli). Choriocarcinoma has NO villi — only sheets of trophoblasts. Invasive mole follows evacuation of a complete mole in ~15% of cases. It can perforate the uterus (causing haemoperitoneum) or embolise to lungs/vagina. BOTH respond well to chemotherapy (usually single-agent for invasive mole)."]','A','The clinical distinction between invasive mole and choriocarcinoma often cannot be made without histological examination (requiring hysterectomy or biopsy). In practice, both are treated based on hCG trajectory and FIGO risk score. Hysterectomy is considered for women who have completed childbearing and have uterus-confined disease, as it reduces chemotherapy courses needed.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Gestational Trophoblastic Disease','medium','flashcard','What is the pathogenesis of the complete hydatidiform mole at the molecular level?','["Complete mole is a purely androgenetic conception — it contains only paternal chromosomes. Mechanism: an enucleate (empty) ovum is fertilised by one sperm (23,X) which duplicates to give 46,XX (most common, ~85%) or by two sperm to give 46,XX or 46,XY. No maternal nuclear genome. Mitochondrial DNA is still maternal. Loss of maternal genomic imprinting means maternally imprinted (paternally expressed) genes are dysregulated, driving trophoblast proliferation. p57KIP2 (CDKN1C) is maternally imprinted — absence of maternal DNA means p57 is NOT expressed (negative IHC staining in complete mole)."]','A','Genomic imprinting explains the phenotype of complete moles: paternal genome drives trophoblast proliferation (evolutionary theory — paternal genes promote placental growth for nutrient extraction); maternal genome supports embryonic development. With only paternal DNA, maximum trophoblastic growth occurs but no embryo forms. This also explains why partial moles (extra paternal set) have excessive trophoblast relative to a normal diploid pregnancy.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Gestational Trophoblastic Disease','hard','flashcard','What are the clinical features that distinguish placental site trophoblastic tumour (PSTT) from choriocarcinoma?','["PSTT: composed of intermediate implantation-site trophoblasts (mononuclear, infiltrating myometrium between muscle fibres); low hCG (only modestly elevated — intermediate trophoblasts produce HPL preferentially); HPL (human placental lactogen) markedly elevated; indolent course; CHEMORESISTANT; treatment = hysterectomy; late metastases possible. Choriocarcinoma: biphasic (cytotrophoblasts + syncytiotrophoblasts); massively elevated hCG; haemorrhagic necrosis; rapidly metastatic; highly CHEMOSENSITIVE (EMA-CO); fertility preservation possible."]','A','PSTT arises months to years after any pregnancy (normal term, abortion, or mole). Its chemoresistance (unlike choriocarcinoma) means hysterectomy is the definitive treatment when disease is confined to the uterus. For metastatic PSTT, EP/EMA is preferred. Epithelioid trophoblastic tumour (ETT) is similar to PSTT but arises from chorionic-type intermediate trophoblasts; also chemoresistant.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Gestational Trophoblastic Disease','hard','flashcard','What is the significance of gestational choriocarcinoma arising after a term pregnancy versus molar pregnancy?','["Choriocarcinoma following term pregnancy: latency period can be months to years after a normal delivery; may be misdiagnosed as postpartum haemorrhage or metastatic disease of unknown primary; most aggressive presentation. Following mole: shorter latency (~6-12 months); detected earlier due to hCG surveillance. Following abortion: intermediate risk. Risk by antecedent: molar pregnancy >> term pregnancy >> ectopic >> miscarriage. Any premenopausal woman with metastatic disease of unknown primary should have hCG measured."]','A','The FIGO risk scoring assigns 2 extra points for a term pregnancy antecedent (vs 0 for molar antecedent), reflecting its worse prognosis when choriocarcinoma follows term delivery. Term-pregnancy choriocarcinoma is often diagnosed late (no hCG monitoring post-delivery in routine practice), at higher stage. Despite this, multi-agent chemotherapy still achieves cure in most cases.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Gestational Trophoblastic Disease','easy','flashcard','What complications can occur at the time of molar pregnancy presentation?','["Complications of complete hydatidiform mole: (1) Hyperemesis gravidarum — markedly elevated hCG stimulates emesis centre and has thyrotrophic activity (hCG cross-reacts with TSH receptor) causing transient hyperthyroidism. (2) Pre-eclampsia before 20 weeks — unique to molar pregnancy (normally pre-eclampsia occurs >20 weeks). (3) Uterine size >dates — due to rapidly proliferating villi filling the cavity. (4) Bilateral theca-lutein cysts (25-30%). (5) Trophoblastic pulmonary emboli — dyspnoea, tachycardia during or after evacuation."]','A','Pre-eclampsia occurring before 20 weeks of gestation should raise the suspicion of molar pregnancy (and also multiple pregnancy, hydrops fetalis). The co-occurrence of hyperthyroidism and pre-eclampsia before 20 weeks in a woman with excessive uterine size and markedly elevated hCG is highly suggestive of complete mole. Urgent TVUS and hCG quantification are diagnostic.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');

-- Subtopic 6: Breast Disease
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Breast Disease','easy','flashcard','What are the risk factors for breast carcinoma?','["Female sex (100x higher risk than men); increasing age (peak 55-65); positive family history (BRCA1/2 — 50-80% lifetime risk); early menarche/late menopause (prolonged oestrogen exposure); nulliparity or first birth >30; HRT (combined oestrogen-progestogen); obesity (postmenopausal — peripheral aromatisation); prior chest irradiation (Hodgkin lymphoma treatment); prior breast atypical hyperplasia or LCIS (4-10x increased risk); dense breast tissue; alcohol consumption."]','A','BRCA1 (chromosome 17q21) and BRCA2 (13q12) are tumour suppressors involved in homologous recombination DNA repair. Germline mutations confer 50-80% lifetime breast cancer risk and 15-40% ovarian cancer risk. Other high-risk genes: PALB2, CHEK2, ATM. Risk-reducing strategies: enhanced surveillance (MRI + mammography), chemoprevention (tamoxifen, anastrozole), prophylactic bilateral mastectomy/salpingo-oophorectomy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Breast Disease','easy','flashcard','What are the intrinsic molecular subtypes of breast cancer and their clinical significance?','["(1) Luminal A: ER+, PR+, HER2-negative, Ki-67 low; best prognosis; responds to endocrine therapy (tamoxifen/AIs); minimal benefit from chemotherapy. (2) Luminal B: ER+, PR±, HER2+/-; Ki-67 high; intermediate prognosis; benefits from chemotherapy + endocrine therapy ± anti-HER2. (3) HER2-enriched: ER-, PR-, HER2+; aggressive without treatment; excellent response to anti-HER2 therapy (trastuzumab, pertuzumab). (4) Triple-negative (basal-like): ER-, PR-, HER2-; most aggressive; no targeted therapy; associated with BRCA1; platinum-based chemotherapy; immunotherapy."]','A','Molecular subtyping guides systemic treatment decisions. Oncotype DX (21-gene recurrence score) and Mammaprint (70-gene signature) are used in ER+/HER2-negative early breast cancer to identify patients who can safely omit chemotherapy. The clinical approximation of molecular subtypes using IHC (ER, PR, HER2, Ki-67) is used in standard practice.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Breast Disease','easy','flashcard','Describe the fibrocystic changes of the breast and their risk implications.','["Fibrocystic change is the most common breast lesion in reproductive-age women. Three components: (1) Cysts: lined by flat epithelium (simple cysts) or apocrine metaplasia (blue dome cysts); (2) Fibrosis: dense stromal collagen; (3) Adenosis: increased acini per lobule. Risk implications depend on epithelial proliferation: Non-proliferative change (cysts, apocrine metaplasia, mild hyperplasia, adenosis) — NO increased cancer risk. Proliferative without atypia (moderate/florid hyperplasia, sclerosing adenosis) — 1.5-2x increased risk. Atypical hyperplasia (ADH, ALH) — 4-5x increased risk (10x if positive family history)."]','A','The DuPont and Page classification of fibrocystic change is the standard for risk stratification. ADH (atypical ductal hyperplasia) fills only part of a duct with cells resembling low-grade DCIS — when similar cells fill the entire duct, the diagnosis becomes low-grade DCIS. ALH (atypical lobular hyperplasia) is a partial involvement of lobules by LCIS-like cells — when >50% of lobules involved, diagnosis is LCIS.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Breast Disease','medium','flashcard','What is DCIS (ductal carcinoma in situ) and how is it classified?','["DCIS: malignant ductal epithelial cells confined within basement membrane, filling and distending ducts. No stromal invasion. Classified by: (1) Nuclear grade: low, intermediate, high. (2) Growth pattern: comedo (central necrosis — high-grade, most aggressive), cribriform, micropapillary, papillary, solid. (3) Presence/absence of necrosis. Van Nuys Prognostic Index uses size, margins, grade, and age to guide treatment. DCIS is not life-threatening itself but is a precursor to invasive carcinoma. Treatment: wide local excision ± radiotherapy ± endocrine therapy (if ER+)."]','A','Comedo-type DCIS is the most aggressive DCIS variant — high nuclear grade, central necrosis with calcification (seen on mammography). It has a higher risk of progression to invasive carcinoma and is treated aggressively. Margin status after excision is critical; positive margins require re-excision. The Van Nuys Index helps decide on radiotherapy vs observation for low-grade DCIS with wide margins.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Breast Disease','medium','flashcard','What is inflammatory breast carcinoma and what is its pathological basis?','["Inflammatory breast carcinoma (IBC): a clinical syndrome characterised by rapid onset of breast erythema, oedema, skin warmth, and peau d''orange (skin dimpling resembling orange peel). It mimics mastitis but does not respond to antibiotics. PATHOLOGICAL BASIS: extensive tumour emboli in dermal lymphatics (not inflammation per se) cause lymphatic obstruction, leading to skin oedema and oedema of subcutaneous fat — creating peau d''orange. Diagnosis requires skin biopsy showing dermal lymphatic tumour emboli. Worst prognosis of all breast cancer subtypes."]','A','IBC accounts for only 2-4% of breast cancers but disproportionately contributes to breast cancer mortality. It is typically ER-negative and HER2-positive or triple-negative. Treatment: neoadjuvant systemic therapy FIRST (not surgery), followed by mastectomy (no BCS), then radiotherapy. Any new breast erythema in a non-lactating woman >6 weeks not resolving with antibiotics should prompt urgent biopsy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Breast Disease','medium','flashcard','Describe the pathological features of invasive lobular carcinoma (ILC) and how they relate to its behaviour.','["ILC: second most common invasive breast cancer (10-15%). Microscopically: discohesive single-file (Indian file) or targetoid (concentric rings around ducts) infiltrating cells with intracytoplasmic mucin vacuoles. Cells are small and bland. CRITICAL FEATURE: loss of E-cadherin (CDH1 mutation or epigenetic silencing) — E-cadherin normally maintains epithelial cell-cell adhesion; its loss causes discohesion. IHC: E-cadherin negative (vs invasive ductal = E-cadherin positive). ER-positive in >90%. Often multifocal/bilateral. Metastasises to unusual sites: meninges, ovaries, GI tract, peritoneum, bone marrow."]','A','E-cadherin loss is the defining molecular feature of lobular neoplasia (LCIS and ILC). ILC is notoriously difficult to detect clinically (no discrete lump — diffuse infiltration) and mammographically (low radiological density). MRI has higher sensitivity. The unusual metastatic pattern (meningeal, peritoneal, GI) reflects the non-cohesive cell behaviour — cells infiltrate singly without forming masses.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Breast Disease','hard','flashcard','What is the role of BRCA1 and BRCA2 in DNA repair and how does their loss drive breast carcinogenesis?','["BRCA1 and BRCA2 proteins are essential components of the homologous recombination (HR) DNA repair pathway. They repair double-strand DNA breaks in a high-fidelity manner. Loss of BRCA1/2 function: (1) Cells cannot repair DSBs via HR; (2) Alternative, error-prone repair pathways (non-homologous end joining) are used, causing genomic instability; (3) Accumulation of mutations in oncogenes and tumour suppressors drives malignant transformation. BRCA1-null tumours are predominantly triple-negative/basal-like, high-grade. BRCA2-null tumours are predominantly ER-positive, similar to sporadic luminal-type cancers."]','A','BRCA deficiency creates a therapeutic vulnerability — PARP inhibitors (olaparib, niraparib) exploit the synthetic lethality concept: PARP enzyme repairs single-strand DNA breaks; when PARP is inhibited in BRCA-deficient cells, unrepaired SSBs convert to DSBs at replication forks, which cannot be repaired by HR, causing cell death. PARP inhibitors are now standard of care for BRCA-mutated breast and ovarian cancers.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Breast Disease','hard','flashcard','What is gynaecomastia and what are its pathological causes?','["Gynaecomastia: benign enlargement of male breast tissue due to proliferation of ducts and stroma (not acini — male breast lacks lobules). Histology: dilated ducts with periductal loose oedematous stroma and periductal lymphocytes; micropapillary ductal epithelial hyperplasia. Causes: (1) Physiological — neonatal (maternal oestrogens), puberty, old age (declining testosterone). (2) Drug-induced — oestrogens, anti-androgens (spironolactone, cimetidine), digoxin, marijuana, anabolic steroids. (3) Diseases — liver cirrhosis (decreased oestrogen metabolism), Klinefelter syndrome, testicular tumours (hCG-producing), hyperthyroidism."]','A','Gynaecomastia must be distinguished from male breast carcinoma: male breast cancer is rare (<1% of all breast cancers), typically presents as a unilateral, firm subareolar mass, often eccentric, sometimes with nipple involvement. BRCA2 mutations confer the highest male breast cancer risk (6% lifetime risk). Gynaecomastia is bilateral, concentric, soft, and diffuse.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Breast Disease','easy','flashcard','What is a fibroadenoma and how does it differ from phyllodes tumour?','["Fibroadenoma: most common benign breast tumour in women <35; biphasic — proliferating ductal epithelium AND fibroblastic stroma; well-circumscribed, mobile (breast mouse); cut surface shows rounded spaces (intracanalicular: stroma compresses epithelium into slit-like spaces; pericanalicular: stroma surrounds rounded glands). Benign, no malignant potential in typical cases. Phyllodes tumour (cystosarcoma phyllodes): similar biphasic structure but with LEAF-LIKE (phyllodes) fronds projecting into cleft-like spaces; stroma is cellular with significant mitotic activity; behaviour: benign, borderline, or malignant based on mitotic count, margins, atypia, stromal overgrowth."]','A','Giant fibroadenoma (>5 cm) can mimic phyllodes tumour. Key histological distinction: phyllodes tumour has stromal cellularity and mitotic activity disproportionate to the epithelial component, leaf-like architecture, and infiltrative margins. Malignant phyllodes has >10 mitoses/HPF, marked atypia, and stromal overgrowth (sarcomatous transformation). Treatment: wide local excision with 1 cm margins for phyllodes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');

-- Subtopic 7: Pregnancy Complications — Early
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Early','easy','flashcard','What are the types of miscarriage and their clinical/pathological features?','["(1) Threatened: bleeding with closed os, viable IUP on US — majority continue (subchorionic haematoma may be seen). (2) Inevitable: bleeding + open os + products felt at os. (3) Incomplete: partial expulsion of POC; open os; tissue in cavity on US; requires evacuation. (4) Complete: all POC expelled; closed os; empty uterus on US; hCG falling. (5) Missed (silent): embryo/fetus dead but retained; no bleeding; US shows absent fetal heartbeat/blighted ovum. (6) Recurrent: ≥3 consecutive losses (investigate for antiphospholipid syndrome, uterine anomaly, parental chromosomal defects, thyroid disease)."]','A','Histopathology of POC shows: chorionic villi (confirms intrauterine pregnancy — important to exclude ectopic), decidua, and in earlier losses, hydropic or degenerating villi. Chromosomal analysis of POC tissue (products of conception) using CGH or FISH identifies causative aneuploidy in ~50% of first-trimester losses, informing counselling and recurrence risk.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Early','easy','flashcard','What is the pathological diagnosis of an ectopic pregnancy in the fallopian tube?','["Ectopic tubal pregnancy: implantation of blastocyst in the fallopian tube (most commonly ampulla). Histology: chorionic villi within tubal wall (often between muscularis layers); decidualised tubal epithelium; no endometrial decidua. The tube cannot expand to accommodate placentation — trophoblastic invasion erodes tubal vessels, causing haemorrhage (haematosalpinx); the tube ruptures at 6-8 weeks (ampullary) or earlier (isthmic — narrower lumen). Haemoperitoneum causes acute abdomen and haemodynamic compromise."]','A','Risk of rupture increases with advancing gestational age. The isthmic ectopic ruptures earliest (smaller lumen) and most dramatically. Interstitial (cornual) ectopic is rare but most dangerous — ruptures at 10-16 weeks (uterine cornua has greater distensibility) with catastrophic haemorrhage. Management: methotrexate (medical, for unruptured, hCG <5000) or surgical (salpingotomy/salpingectomy).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Early','medium','flashcard','Describe the chromosomal abnormalities most commonly found in first-trimester miscarriage.','["Chromosomal abnormalities (~50-60% of first-trimester losses): Most common — autosomal trisomies (60% of chromosomally abnormal losses); Trisomy 16 is most frequent (incompatible with life); Trisomies 13, 18, 21 may also survive to second trimester. Monosomy X (45,X): 15-20% of abnormal losses (Turner syndrome — most 45,X pregnancies miscarry). Triploidy (69,XXX or 69,XXY): 15-20%; produces partial mole or hydropic fetus. Tetraploidy: rare; very early loss. Structural anomalies (translocations): 5%; identifies carriers needing preimplantation genetic testing in future pregnancies."]','A','The high frequency of chromosomal errors in miscarriage represents natural selection removing severely abnormal conceptuses. Most sporadic miscarriages are caused by de novo chromosomal errors — the recurrence risk is low (~5% for one, ~25% for 3+ losses). Parental karyotyping is recommended after ≥3 recurrent losses to detect balanced translocations (risk of unbalanced offspring).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Early','medium','flashcard','What is antiphospholipid syndrome (APS) and how does it cause pregnancy loss?','["APS: autoimmune condition with persistent antiphospholipid antibodies (lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin antibodies, anti-beta2-glycoprotein I) causing thrombosis and/or pregnancy morbidity. Sapporo/Sydney criteria require both: clinical (thrombosis or pregnancy morbidity — ≥1 unexplained loss ≥10 weeks, or ≥3 early losses <10 weeks, or severe pre-eclampsia/placental insufficiency) AND persistent antibodies ≥12 weeks apart. Pathological mechanism: placental thrombosis and infarction from aPL-induced hypercoagulability, complement activation, and direct trophoblast dysfunction. Treatment: LMWH + low-dose aspirin."]','A','APS is the most important treatable cause of recurrent pregnancy loss. LMWH + aspirin increases live birth rates from ~10-20% (untreated) to ~70-80%. Catastrophic APS (CAPS) is a rare life-threatening multi-organ thrombotic crisis requiring anticoagulation, corticosteroids, plasma exchange, and IVIG.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Early','medium','flashcard','What distinguishes a hydropic abortion from a partial or complete mole?','["Hydropic abortion: villous oedema (hydrops) from non-molar causes (chromosomal trisomy, fetal death, or hydrops fetalis); p57KIP2 positive (maternal genome present); ploidy usually diploid or aneuploid; no trophoblastic hyperplasia. Partial mole: triploid (69,XXX or 69,XXY); p57 positive; focal trophoblastic hyperplasia and villous scalloping (irregular outlines); fetal tissue may be present. Complete mole: diploid androgenetic; p57 NEGATIVE; diffuse trophoblastic hyperplasia; all villi hydropic; no fetal tissue. Distinction is critical because only moles require hCG surveillance."]','A','p57KIP2 IHC performed on all POC with hydrops is now standard practice in pathology. This simple test prevents misclassification of hydropic abortions as moles (avoiding unnecessary hCG surveillance) and prevents complete moles being missed (avoiding failure to surveil). Flow cytometry for ploidy is a complementary test used in difficult cases.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Early','hard','flashcard','What are the pathological findings in recurrent implantation failure and how do they differ from recurrent miscarriage?','["Recurrent implantation failure (RIF): failure of ≥3 good-quality embryos to implant in IVF cycles. Pathological causes: (1) Endometrial receptivity defects — chronic endometritis (plasma cell infiltrate on Noyes criteria — normally no plasma cells; CD138 IHC used); thin endometrium (<7mm); poor Window of Implantation (WOI — displaced implantation window assessed by endometrial receptivity array/ERA test); (2) Anatomical — submucosal fibroids, polyps, intrauterine adhesions; (3) Thrombophilia (APS, MTHFR); (4) Embryo quality issues. Different from recurrent miscarriage (RPL) which implies confirmed IUP then loss."]','A','Chronic endometritis (CE) is an underdiagnosed cause of both RPL and RIF. Plasma cells are not normally found in the endometrium; their presence (>5 CD138+ cells/high-power field) confirms CE. It is often caused by subclinical chlamydial or mycoplasmal infection and responds to doxycycline or a fluoroquinolone. Treatment of CE significantly improves IVF outcomes in women with RIF.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Early','hard','flashcard','Describe the pathological changes in the fallopian tube in ectopic pregnancy and salpingitis.','["Ectopic pregnancy in tube: trophoblastic invasion between tubal muscularis layers; haematosalpinx (blood in tubal lumen); villi may perforate through the serosa. Chorionic villi in tubal wall are diagnostic. Acute salpingitis: neutrophilic infiltrate in tubal mucosa and wall; luminal pus (pyosalpinx); mucosal plicae preserved initially. Chronic salpingitis: lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate; fusion and flattening of mucosal folds; fibrosis; follicular salpingitis (lymphoid follicles in wall). Hydrosalpinx: chronic obstruction → fluid accumulation → dilated thin-walled tube; mucosal folds blunted and flattened."]','A','The pathological progression from acute salpingitis → chronic salpingitis → hydrosalpinx mirrors the clinical progression from acute PID to chronic pelvic pain to infertility. Salpingitis isthmica nodosa (SIN) — nodular thickening of the isthmic tube with gland-like spaces within the muscularis — is a distinct entity associated with ectopic pregnancy and infertility, caused by progressive smooth muscle hypertrophy trapping epithelial invaginations.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Early','easy','flashcard','What are the clinical features and management of a ruptured ectopic pregnancy?','["Ruptured ectopic: acute surgical emergency. Symptoms: sudden severe unilateral pelvic/iliac fossa pain radiating to shoulder tip (diaphragmatic irritation from haemoperitoneum); vaginal bleeding (usually less than expected for degree of shock); history of missed period + positive pregnancy test. Signs: haemodynamic instability (tachycardia, hypotension, pallor), peritonism, cervical excitation (CMT — cervical motion tenderness). Investigations: urgent TVUS (haemoperitoneum + empty uterus), serum beta-hCG. Management: immediate IV access, fluid resuscitation, emergency laparoscopy/laparotomy (salpingectomy or salpingotomy)."]','A','Cervical excitation (cervical motion tenderness / chandelier sign) on bimanual examination is a key clinical finding in ruptured ectopic — moving the cervix causes pain by disturbing the haemoperitoneum. Shoulder-tip pain (referred from C3/C4 phrenic nerve irritation) occurs when blood tracks to the diaphragm. Death from ruptured ectopic is preventable with early diagnosis and access to surgical care.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Early','medium','flashcard','What is heterotopic pregnancy and why is it clinically challenging?','["Heterotopic pregnancy: simultaneous intrauterine and ectopic pregnancy. Incidence: 1:30,000 spontaneous conceptions; 1:100-500 with IVF/ovulation induction (due to multiple embryo transfer and stimulation of multiple follicles). Clinical challenge: the presence of a confirmed IUP on ultrasound does NOT exclude a concurrent ectopic pregnancy. The pain and haemoperitoneum of the ectopic may be attributed to the IUP or vice versa. The ectopic requires treatment (surgery preferred in heterotopic — methotrexate would harm the IUP) while protecting the intrauterine pregnancy."]','A','Heterotopic pregnancy is the classic pitfall in ectopic pregnancy diagnosis — clinicians reassured by seeing an IUP on ultrasound may miss a coexisting ectopic. All women with IVF pregnancies and abdominal pain must have a thorough ultrasound examination of the adnexa even when an IUP is confirmed. This highlights the importance of scanning both adnexa routinely in all early pregnancy assessments.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');

-- Subtopic 8: Pregnancy Complications — Late
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Late','easy','flashcard','Describe the pathogenesis of pre-eclampsia in three stages.','["Stage 1 — Poor placentation (6-18 weeks): inadequate extravillous trophoblast invasion of spiral arteries; arteries remain high-resistance muscular vessels instead of becoming dilated low-resistance uteroplacental vessels. Stage 2 — Placental oxidative stress (>18 weeks): ischaemic placenta releases excess anti-angiogenic factors (sFlt-1, sEng) into maternal circulation, causing endothelial dysfunction. Stage 3 — Maternal syndrome (>20 weeks): systemic endothelial injury → hypertension (vasospasm), proteinuria (glomerular endotheliosis), oedema, HELLP syndrome, eclampsia (seizures)."]','A','The imbalance between pro-angiogenic (VEGF, PlGF) and anti-angiogenic factors (sFlt-1, soluble endoglin) is central to pre-eclampsia pathophysiology. Elevated sFlt-1 and reduced PlGF precede clinical pre-eclampsia by weeks. The sFlt-1/PlGF ratio (<38 = pre-eclampsia unlikely in the next week; >85 = high risk) is now used clinically as a predictive/diagnostic biomarker (Elecsys sFlt-1/PlGF).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Late','easy','flashcard','Compare placenta praevia and placental abruption clinically and pathologically.','["Placenta praevia: painless antepartum haemorrhage; placenta overlies internal os on US; soft non-tender uterus; no fetal compromise initially; causes: previous C-section, multiparity; digital VE contraindicated; management: delivery by C-section. Placental abruption: painful haemorrhage (retroplacental haematoma — may be concealed); rigid tender uterus; fetal distress/death; causes: hypertension, cocaine, trauma, thrombophilia, PPROM; pathology: retroplacental haematoma; may cause DIC (tissue factor release); management: delivery + DIC management."]','A','Placental abruption can be classified as revealed (external bleeding), concealed (retroplacental haematoma only, most dangerous), or mixed. Concealed abruption is more dangerous as the extent of haemorrhage is underestimated. The Kleihauer-Betke test detects fetomaternal haemorrhage. Recurrence risk: 10-15% after one abruption, rising to 25% after two.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Late','medium','flashcard','What are the criteria for diagnosis of HELLP syndrome and how is it managed?','["HELLP syndrome diagnostic criteria (Tennessee classification): H = Haemolysis (abnormal peripheral blood smear — schistocytes, spherocytes; elevated LDH ≥600 IU/L; elevated indirect bilirubin; low haptoglobin); EL = Elevated Liver enzymes (AST/ALT ≥2x upper limit of normal); LP = Low Platelets (<100 x10^9/L for class 1, 50-100 for class 2, >50 for class 3 Mississippi). Associated with RUQ/epigastric pain, nausea, severe hypertension. Complications: subcapsular hepatic haematoma (risk of rupture), DIC, acute kidney injury. Management: delivery (definitive); corticosteroids (betamethasone) for fetal lung maturity; magnesium sulphate for seizure prophylaxis."]','A','HELLP syndrome represents severe end-organ damage in pre-eclampsia. Thrombocytopaenia results from microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia (MAHA) — platelet consumption in damaged vessels. Hepatic involvement (periportal haemorrhagic necrosis on biopsy) causes elevated transaminases. Subcapsular hepatic haematoma requires urgent delivery and may rupture catastrophically. DIC management requires FFP, cryoprecipitate, and platelet transfusion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Late','medium','flashcard','What are the placental pathological features associated with intrauterine fetal growth restriction (IUGR)?','["IUGR-associated placental pathology: (1) Infarcts — areas of ischaemic villous necrosis (fibrinoid deposits, ghost villi); multiple/confluent infarcts indicate uteroplacental insufficiency; (2) Decidual vasculopathy — incomplete spiral artery remodelling, fibrinoid necrosis, acute atherosis (foam cells, fibrinoid, lymphocytes in vessel walls — resembles atherosclerosis); (3) Increased syncytial knots (Tenney-Parker change) — syncytiotrophoblast accumulates at villous periphery in chronic hypoxia; (4) Villous dysmaturity — abnormal villous maturation; (5) Retroplacental haematoma (if abruption)."]','A','Placental examination (gross and histological) after delivery provides retrospective diagnosis of the underlying cause of IUGR, stillbirth, or neonatal compromise. Perivillous fibrin deposition, massive fibrin deposition (MFD), and maternal floor infarction are distinct entities causing recurrent severe IUGR. The Amsterdam criteria provide standardised nomenclature for placental pathology reporting.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Late','medium','flashcard','What is eclampsia and what are its pathological brain findings?','["Eclampsia: new-onset grand mal seizures (tonic-clonic) in a woman with pre-eclampsia, not attributable to other causes. Occurs ante-, intra-, or post-partum (up to 6 weeks post-partum). Brain pathological findings: (1) Cerebral oedema (vasogenic from endothelial dysfunction); (2) Microhaemorrhages and petechiae throughout cortex; (3) Fibrinoid necrosis of small vessels; (4) Cortical and subcortical haemorrhagic infarcts. PRES (posterior reversible encephalopathy syndrome) on MRI: symmetrical T2/FLAIR hyperintensity in posterior parieto-occipital regions (vasogenic oedema). Magnesium sulphate is the anticonvulsant of choice."]','A','Magnesium sulphate (MgSO4) prevents and treats eclamptic seizures via: inhibition of NMDA glutamate receptors, cerebrovascular vasodilation, and membrane stabilisation. It is superior to phenytoin and diazepam for eclampsia prevention/treatment. The Magpie trial demonstrated 50% reduction in seizure risk with prophylactic MgSO4 in severe pre-eclampsia. Serum Mg monitoring is essential (therapeutic: 2-3.5 mmol/L; toxicity: loss of reflexes >3.5 mmol/L; respiratory arrest >5 mmol/L).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Late','hard','flashcard','Describe the pathological spectrum of placenta accreta spectrum (PAS) disorders.','["Placenta accreta spectrum (PAS): abnormal placental adherence/invasion due to absent or defective decidua basalis. Three grades: (1) Accreta (75%): chorionic villi attach directly to myometrium (no decidua); (2) Increta (18%): villi invade into myometrium; (3) Percreta (7%): villi penetrate through myometrium to or through serosa; may invade bladder/bowel. PRIMARY RISK FACTOR: previous uterine scar (especially combined with placenta praevia — risk of PAS >60% with ≥3 previous C-sections). Pathogenesis: defective decidualisation at scar site prevents normal cytotrophoblast detachment at delivery."]','A','PAS is associated with massive obstetric haemorrhage at delivery (placenta fails to separate — manual removal attempted → torrential bleeding from open placental bed). Management requires a multidisciplinary team (obstetrician, urologist, vascular surgeon, haematologist, anaesthetist) at a specialist centre. Options: planned C-section hysterectomy (definitive), or conservative management (leaving placenta in situ with methotrexate — risk of infection and delayed haemorrhage).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Late','hard','flashcard','What is acute fatty liver of pregnancy (AFLP) and what are its distinguishing features from other liver diseases in pregnancy?','["AFLP: third-trimester emergency caused by microvesicular fatty infiltration of hepatocytes from impaired mitochondrial beta-oxidation (LCHAD deficiency — long-chain 3-hydroxyacyl-CoA dehydrogenase; fetal defect, maternal carrier). Presents 30-38 weeks: nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, jaundice, hypoglycaemia, coagulopathy, encephalopathy. Labs: elevated transaminases, elevated bilirubin, prolonged PT/APTT, low glucose, low fibrinogen (DIC), thrombocytopaenia. Biopsy: microvesicular fat in hepatocytes (spares cell nucleus — diffuse). Delivery is life-saving. Distinguish from: HELLP (elevated AST/ALT but not liver failure; no hypoglycaemia); obstetric cholestasis (pruritis, elevated bile acids, normal synthetic function)."]','A','AFLP is associated with male fetus, twin pregnancy, and primigravida status. Mortality was historically >80%; now <5% with early recognition and urgent delivery. LCHAD deficiency in the fetus causes accumulation of long-chain 3-hydroxy fatty acid metabolites that cross the placenta and overwhelm the heterozygous mother''s deficient mitochondrial beta-oxidation capacity. The infant must be tested for LCHAD deficiency and managed with a special formula.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Late','easy','flashcard','What are the risk factors for pre-eclampsia?','["High-risk factors (one sufficient for aspirin prophylaxis): previous pre-eclampsia; chronic hypertension; chronic kidney disease; diabetes mellitus (type 1 or 2); autoimmune disease (SLE, APS); multiple pregnancy. Moderate-risk factors (two or more needed): nulliparity; BMI >35; age >40; inter-pregnancy interval >10 years; family history of pre-eclampsia; booking diastolic BP 80-99 mmHg. Combined first-trimester screening (MAP + uterine artery PI + PIGF) identifies 90%+ at high risk (ASPRE algorithm). Low-dose aspirin (150 mg nocte) from 12-16 weeks to 36 weeks significantly reduces pre-eclampsia risk in high-risk women."]','A','NICE and FIGO guidelines recommend aspirin 75-150 mg/day from 11-14 weeks in high-risk women. The mechanism: aspirin inhibits thromboxane A2 (platelet aggregation and vasoconstriction) more than prostacyclin (vasodilation), improving uteroplacental blood flow. The ASPRE trial showed 62% reduction in preterm pre-eclampsia with 150 mg aspirin commenced <16 weeks in high-risk pregnancies.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Late','medium','flashcard','What is vasa praevia and why is it particularly dangerous?','["Vasa praevia: fetal blood vessels (from umbilical cord or accessory/velamentous cord insertion) run unsupported through the membranes overlying or adjacent to the internal cervical os. Classification: Type 1 — velamentous cord insertion with vessels crossing the os; Type 2 — vessels connecting a succenturiate lobe to the main placenta crossing the os. DANGER: when membranes rupture (spontaneously or artificially), unprotected fetal vessels tear → rapid exsanguination of the fetus (total fetal blood volume ~250-300 mL). Mortality approaches 100% if undiagnosed; 97% survival with antenatal diagnosis and planned C-section at 34-36 weeks."]','A','Vasa praevia presents as sudden onset painless vaginal haemorrhage at membrane rupture, with immediate fetal bradycardia/sinusoidal trace. Apt test (Kleihauer-Betke or Lovaas test on vaginal blood) confirms fetal haemoglobin. Antenatal diagnosis by TVUS colour Doppler showing vessels over the os is the key to preventing fetal death. High-risk pregnancies (bilobed placenta, low-lying placenta, IVF, velamentous cord) should be screened at 20-week anomaly scan.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');

-- Subtopic 9: Male Reproductive Pathology
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Male Reproductive Pathology','easy','flashcard','Classify testicular germ cell tumours with their tumour markers.','["Two main groups: (1) SEMINOMA: large, uniform cells; AFP always NEGATIVE (elevated AFP excludes pure seminoma); beta-hCG mildly elevated in 10%; LDH elevated; PLAP (placental alkaline phosphatase) positive; highly radiosensitive and chemosensitive; excellent prognosis. (2) NON-SEMINOMATOUS GCT (NSGCT): includes embryonal carcinoma (AFP±, hCG±), yolk sac tumour (AFP markedly elevated), choriocarcinoma (beta-hCG markedly elevated, AFP negative), teratoma (AFP/hCG variable), and mixed tumours. AFP elevation = yolk sac component. All GCTs share i(12p) chromosomal abnormality."]','A','The key clinical rule: an elevated AFP in a man with a testicular mass EXCLUDES pure seminoma and mandates treatment as NSGCT (even if histology appears seminomatous — it means yolk sac component was missed). All three markers (AFP, beta-hCG, LDH) must be measured pre-orchiectomy as baseline for staging and response monitoring. BEP (bleomycin, etoposide, cisplatin) is the standard chemotherapy for GCT.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Male Reproductive Pathology','easy','flashcard','Describe the pathology of benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH).','["BPH: non-malignant enlargement of the prostate affecting the transition zone (periurethral glands). Pathology: hyperplasia of both glandular (acinar) and stromal (smooth muscle and fibrous) elements — nodular hyperplasia. Glands are enlarged, lined by two cell layers (luminal columnar secretory cells + basal cells), may contain corpora amylacea (laminated calcifications). Obstruction: compressed prostatic urethra causes LUTS (lower urinary tract symptoms): hesitancy, weak stream, terminal dribbling, incomplete emptying, nocturia, urgency. Secondary changes: detrusor hypertrophy → trabeculation → diverticula → hydronephrosis."]','A','BPH is driven by dihydrotestosterone (DHT — converted from testosterone by 5-alpha-reductase in prostatic stroma). 5-alpha-reductase inhibitors (finasteride, dutasteride) reduce prostate volume and LUTS by blocking DHT production. Alpha-1 adrenoreceptor blockers (tamsulosin, alfuzosin) relax smooth muscle in the prostate and bladder neck, rapidly improving urine flow. Surgical options (TURP) for refractory cases.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Male Reproductive Pathology','easy','flashcard','What are the Gleason grades for prostate carcinoma and what does Gleason score mean?','["Gleason grading (1-5) based on glandular architecture: Grade 1: well-formed, round, closely-packed glands (very rare in biopsies). Grade 2: loosely arranged glands. Grade 3: discrete infiltrating glands (most commonly seen on biopsy). Grade 4: fused/cribriform glands or poorly formed glands. Grade 5: no gland formation — single cells, cords, solid sheets. Gleason Score (GS) = Primary grade (most common pattern) + Secondary grade (second most common) = 2-10. Clinical groups: GS 6 = favourable low risk; GS 7 (3+4 = Grade Group 2; 4+3 = Grade Group 3); GS 8 = Grade Group 4; GS 9-10 = Grade Group 5 (highest risk)."]','A','Grade Groups 1-5 (ISUP 2014) replaced the old Gleason score terminology for clearer prognostic communication. Grade Group 1 (GS ≤6): indolent, active surveillance appropriate. Grade Group 5 (GS 9-10): aggressive, systemic therapy needed. The proportion of Gleason 4 in GS 7 tumours (3+4 vs 4+3) significantly impacts prognosis — 4+3 has higher biochemical recurrence rate than 3+4.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Male Reproductive Pathology','medium','flashcard','What is cryptorchidism and what are its complications?','["Cryptorchidism (undescended testis): failure of testis to descend from retroperitoneum into scrotum during development. Present in 3% of term, 30% of preterm males; most descend by age 3 months (only 1% remain undescended at age 1). Complications: (1) Infertility — spermatogenesis requires 2-4°C below core body temperature; intra-abdominal testis undergoes progressive tubular atrophy/hyalinisation (Sertoli cell only pattern). (2) Testicular cancer — 3-5x increased risk of GCT (seminoma > NSGCT); risk persists even after orchidopexy, though reduced. (3) Torsion — abnormal fixation predisposes. (4) Psychological — absent scrotal contents."]','A','Orchidopexy (surgical fixation into scrotum) is recommended before age 18 months to preserve fertility potential (window closes as tubular atrophy progresses) and allow easier self-examination for cancer detection. The contralateral descended testis in unilateral cryptorchidism also has increased cancer risk — suggesting a bilateral germ cell environment abnormality rather than purely mechanical (temperature) cause.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Male Reproductive Pathology','medium','flashcard','What is germ cell neoplasia in situ (GCNIS) and its significance?','["GCNIS (previously called carcinoma in situ/intratubular germ cell neoplasia/ITGCN): neoplastic germ cells with large nuclei, prominent nucleoli, and clear glycogen-rich cytoplasm confined within seminiferous tubules, replacing normal spermatogonia along the basement membrane. Tubules show Sertoli cell only without normal spermatogenesis. GCNIS is the precursor to both seminoma and NSGCT. Present in cryptorchid testes, contralateral testis of GCT patients (5%), and infertile men (0.5-1%). Detected by biopsy; treated with local testicular irradiation (20 Gy) to prevent invasive GCT if fertility preservation desired, or orchiectomy."]','A','GCNIS cells express OCT3/4 (POU5F1), PLAP, and c-KIT, reflecting their undifferentiated germ cell nature. i(12p) is present in GCNIS, confirming its role as the direct precursor. The 5-year risk of progression from GCNIS to invasive GCT is ~50% — all detected GCNIS requires treatment. Sperm banking before radiotherapy/orchiectomy is essential for men desiring fertility.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Male Reproductive Pathology','medium','flashcard','Compare the histological features of seminoma, embryonal carcinoma, and yolk sac tumour.','["Seminoma: sheets of large uniform cells with clear glycogen-rich cytoplasm, central vesicular nuclei with prominent nucleoli; fibrous septa with lymphocytic infiltrate; granulomatous reaction; AFP negative, PLAP positive. Embryonal carcinoma: pleomorphic anaplastic cells with indistinct borders, glandular/papillary/solid patterns; high mitotic rate; haemorrhagic necrosis; OCT3/4+, CD30+, AFP variable, PLAP variable; most aggressive of pure types. Yolk sac tumour: reticular/lacy (microcystic) pattern with Schiller-Duval bodies (glomeruloid perivascular structures); AFP markedly elevated; alpha-1-antitrypsin+; most common GCT in infants."]','A','IHC panel for GCTs: OCT3/4 (POU5F1) — positive in seminoma and embryonal carcinoma, negative in teratoma/yolk sac/choriocarcinoma. CD117 (c-KIT) — seminoma only. CD30 — embryonal carcinoma only. AFP — yolk sac tumour. hCG — choriocarcinoma (syncytiotrophoblasts). SALL4 — all GCTs. This panel guides diagnosis in metastatic disease without primary identified.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Male Reproductive Pathology','hard','flashcard','What is the pathological difference between prostate carcinoma and PIN (prostatic intraepithelial neoplasia)?','["High-grade PIN (HGPIN): architecturally normal or slightly enlarged glands/ducts with markedly atypical cells (large nuclei, prominent macronucleoli resembling invasive carcinoma), but basal cell layer INTACT (p63+, CK5/6+ by IHC). Invasive prostate carcinoma: small glands with markedly atypical cells, NO basal cell layer (p63 negative); single cell layer (luminal cells only); may show perineural invasion (diagnostic of carcinoma); nucleomegaly with macronucleoli; AMACR (alpha-methylacyl-CoA racemase/P504S) positive in carcinoma and PIN. Key IHC panel: p63 + CK5/6 + AMACR — basal cells absent (p63-) + AMACR+ = carcinoma."]','A','The basal cell layer is the key discriminating feature between PIN and invasive carcinoma. In routine H&E, the basal cell layer may not be clearly visible; IHC for p63 or CK5/6 (basal cell markers) demonstrates its presence in PIN and absence in carcinoma. AMACR overexpression occurs in ~80% of prostate cancers and ~15-20% of HGPINs. Partial absence of basal cells or AMACR positivity in architecturally suspicious glands = atypical glands suspicious for carcinoma (ASAP).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Male Reproductive Pathology','hard','flashcard','What hormonal changes occur in prostate carcinoma and how does androgen deprivation therapy (ADT) work?','["Prostate carcinoma is androgen-dependent — testosterone and DHT drive tumour cell proliferation via androgen receptor (AR) signalling. ADT strategies: (1) GnRH agonists (leuprolide, goserelin) — initial surge then downregulation of pituitary LH/FSH, reducing testosterone to castrate levels (<50 ng/dL). (2) GnRH antagonists (degarelix, relugolix) — immediate LH/FSH suppression without flare. (3) Anti-androgens (bicalutamide, enzalutamide) — block AR directly. (4) CYP17A1 inhibitors (abiraterone) — block testosterone synthesis in adrenal glands, testes, and tumour. Castration-resistant prostate carcinoma (CRPC) develops via AR amplification, AR splice variants (AR-V7), or AR-independent pathways."]','A','ADT is the backbone of treatment for locally advanced and metastatic prostate carcinoma. Combined androgen blockade (CAB = GnRH agonist + anti-androgen) provides maximal androgen suppression. After 18-24 months, most metastatic tumours develop CRPC — rising PSA despite castrate testosterone. Second-generation hormonal agents (enzalutamide, abiraterone, darolutamide) and taxane chemotherapy (docetaxel, cabazitaxel) are used in CRPC.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Male Reproductive Pathology','easy','flashcard','What is epididymo-orchitis and what are its common causative organisms by age group?','["Epididymo-orchitis: inflammation of the epididymis (epididymitis) ± testis (orchitis). Symptoms: painful scrotal swelling, erythema, dysuria, fever; epididymis tender on palpation. Causes by age: Under 35 years: STI — Chlamydia trachomatis (most common), Neisseria gonorrhoeae; urethral discharge common. Over 35 years: urinary tract organisms — E. coli (gram-negative rods); associated with BPH, urinary tract instrumentation, prostatitis. Special: Mumps orchitis (viral — bilateral in 30%): occurs in post-pubertal males; can cause testicular atrophy and infertility; Brucella, TB (granulomatous orchitis in endemic areas). Treatment: doxycycline (Chlamydia); ceftriaxone + doxycycline (gonococcal); ciprofloxacin (enteric)."]','A','Distinguishing epididymo-orchitis from testicular torsion is critical — both present with acute scrotal pain. Features favouring orchitis: gradual onset, fever, dysuria, urethral discharge, positive urine culture, preserved blood flow on Doppler US. Features favouring torsion: sudden onset, younger age (10-30), absent cremasteric reflex, transverse-lying testis, high-riding testis on exam, absent flow on Doppler — surgical emergency.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');

-- Subtopic 10: Sexually Transmitted Infections — Pathology
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Sexually Transmitted Infections — Pathology','easy','flashcard','Describe the three stages of syphilis and their pathological features.','["Primary syphilis (10-90 days after exposure): painless indurated chancre at inoculation site; histology — plasma cell-rich perivascular infiltrate, obliterative endarteritis; heals in 4-6 weeks. Secondary syphilis (2-8 weeks after chancre heals): disseminated disease — maculopapular rash on trunk/palms/soles, condylomata lata (moist flat plaques in perineum), lymphadenopathy, oral snail track ulcers; histology — obliterative endarteritis; highly infectious. Tertiary syphilis (years later): gummas (granulomatous lesions with central necrosis, plasma cells, endarteritis — skin, bone, liver), cardiovascular syphilis (aortitis — tree bark aorta, aortic regurgitation, coronary ostial stenosis), neurosyphilis (tabes dorsalis, meningovascular, general paresis of the insane)."]','A','Obliterative endarteritis (endothelial cell proliferation in small vessel walls) is the pathological hallmark seen in ALL stages of syphilis. In cardiovascular syphilis: endarteritis of the vasa vasorum of the ascending aorta causes ischaemic medial damage → weakening → aneurysm (aneurysm of the ascending aorta/arch is almost pathognomonic of syphilis in the pre-antibiotic era). Tree-bark appearance from fibrous scarring and wrinkling.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Sexually Transmitted Infections — Pathology','easy','flashcard','Describe the pathology and clinical features of gonorrhoea.','["Gonorrhoea (Neisseria gonorrhoeae — gram-negative diplococci, intracellular): Incubation 2-7 days. In men: acute purulent urethritis (urethral discharge, dysuria); complications: epididymo-orchitis, urethral stricture, prostatitis. In women: often asymptomatic; cervicitis (mucopurulent discharge); ascending infection → acute salpingitis (PID) → tubo-ovarian abscess → Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome (perihepatitis — violin string adhesions); complications: infertility, ectopic pregnancy. Histology: acute inflammatory exudate with neutrophils; gram-negative diplococci within neutrophil cytoplasm (intracellular). Disseminated gonococcal infection (DGI): bacteraemia → septic arthritis, skin pustules, tenosynovitis."]','A','N. gonorrhoeae evades immune clearance by antigenic variation of pili and Opa proteins. It uses IgA protease to destroy secretory IgA in mucosal surfaces. Treatment: dual therapy — ceftriaxone IM (ESBL concerns making oral options less reliable) + azithromycin (or doxycycline for chlamydia co-infection). N. gonorrhoeae is developing resistance to multiple antibiotic classes (including fluoroquinolones and third-generation cephalosporins).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Sexually Transmitted Infections — Pathology','easy','flashcard','What are the pathological manifestations of HSV genital infection?','["HSV-2 (mainly) and HSV-1 cause genital herpes. Primary infection: multiple painful vesicles on erythematous base → ulcerate → crust → heal (2-4 weeks); inguinal lymphadenopathy; systemic symptoms (fever, malaise — more severe than recurrences). Pathology: intraepidermal vesicles with ballooning degeneration of keratinocytes; Tzanck cells (multinucleated giant cells with Cowdry A intranuclear inclusions — eosinophilic inclusions with clear halo); lymphocytic infiltrate. Recurrences: less severe, triggered by stress/immunosuppression; virus latent in sacral dorsal root ganglia (DRG). Neonatal herpes: acquired during delivery — disseminated HSV causing encephalitis (mortality 80% untreated)."]','A','HSV establishes latency in dorsal root ganglia (trigeminal for oral HSV; sacral for genital HSV). Reactivation travels anterograde down peripheral nerve to skin, causing vesicular outbreak. Diagnosis: PCR of vesicle fluid (most sensitive), Tzanck smear (low sensitivity, rapid), HSV type-specific serology (IgG — evidence of past infection). Treatment: aciclovir/valaciclovir (nucleoside analogue that inhibits HSV DNA polymerase); suppressive therapy in frequent recurrences.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Sexually Transmitted Infections — Pathology','medium','flashcard','What is pelvic inflammatory disease (PID) and what are its long-term pathological consequences?','["PID: ascending infection from cervix/vagina to endometrium (endometritis), fallopian tubes (salpingitis), ovaries (oophoritis), and peritoneum (peritonitis). Causative organisms: Chlamydia trachomatis (most common — often silent), Neisseria gonorrhoeae, polymicrobial (Bacteroides, E. coli, Streptococcus). Acute PID: neutrophilic salpingitis; pyosalpinx (pus in tube); tubo-ovarian abscess. Long-term pathological consequences: (1) Tubal factor infertility (35% after one episode, 75% after three); (2) Ectopic pregnancy (7-10x increased risk); (3) Chronic pelvic pain (18%); (4) Hydrosalpinx; (5) Perihepatitis (Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome — 5-15%); (6) Increased risk of ovarian cancer (controversial)."]','A','The Fitz-Hugh-Curtis syndrome (perihepatitis) causes right upper quadrant pain (mimicking cholecystitis), caused by Chlamydia or gonococcal peritoneal spread to the liver capsule forming fibrous adhesions between the liver surface and anterior abdominal wall (violin string or piano wire adhesions). Liver parenchyma is unaffected; LFTs are normal. Laparoscopy is diagnostic.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Sexually Transmitted Infections — Pathology','medium','flashcard','What are the serological tests for syphilis and how are they interpreted?','["Treponemal tests (specific for T. pallidum): TPPA (T. pallidum particle agglutination), FTA-ABS, TPHA — remain positive for life after infection (do NOT indicate active disease alone; cannot distinguish treated from untreated). Non-treponemal tests (cardiolipin antibodies): RPR (rapid plasma reagin), VDRL — quantitative titre; become positive 1-4 weeks after chancre; decrease and become negative with successful treatment; used to monitor treatment response and detect reinfection. Modern algorithm: treponemal test (EIA/CLIA) first (high sensitivity) → if positive, confirm with RPR titre + second treponemal test. VDRL in CSF: gold standard for neurosyphilis diagnosis."]','A','Biological false-positive RPR/VDRL (non-treponemal positive but treponemal negative) occurs in: SLE, antiphospholipid syndrome, pregnancy, viral infections (EBV, HIV), old age, malaria. The quantitative RPR titre is the key monitoring tool: successful treatment → fourfold (two dilution) fall in titre within 6-12 months. Failure to decline suggests treatment failure, re-infection, or neurosyphilis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Sexually Transmitted Infections — Pathology','medium','flashcard','What is chancroid (soft chancre) and how does it differ from the syphilitic chancre?','["Chancroid (Haemophilus ducreyi): Painful, soft, ragged-edged ulcer with necrotic exudate and undermined edges; NOT indurated; inguinal lymphadenopathy (bubo) that may suppurate. Pathology: acute inflammatory exudate; gram-negative coccobacilli in chains (school of fish). Syphilitic chancre: painless, indurated (hard), clean base, smooth edge; heals without treatment; no pus. Key distinction: SOFT (chancroid) vs HARD (syphilis) ulcer; PAINFUL (chancroid, herpes) vs PAINLESS (syphilis, LGV initial lesion). Both increase HIV transmission risk. Chancroid is now rare in high-income countries but remains significant in Sub-Saharan Africa and Asia."]','A','Co-infection with multiple STIs is common. Any genital ulcer increases HIV transmission risk 10-100-fold by disrupting mucosal integrity and attracting HIV-susceptible CD4+ T cells. The 3 main causes of genital ulcer disease (GUD): syphilis (painless, indurated), herpes (painful, multiple vesicles/ulcers), chancroid (painful, soft, necrotic). In resource-limited settings, syndromic management treats all three simultaneously.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Sexually Transmitted Infections — Pathology','hard','flashcard','Describe the pathology of congenital syphilis.','["Congenital syphilis: transplacental transmission of T. pallidum from an infected mother (primary, secondary, or early latent syphilis — 80-90% transmission rate). Placental findings: placentomegaly, villous oedema, perivascular inflammation, spirochaetes on Warthin-Starry stain. Early congenital syphilis (<2 years): hepatosplenomegaly (liver infiltrated with spirochaetes and plasma cells), jaundice, pneumonia alba (white hepatisation of lungs), rhinitis (snuffles), anaemia, osteochondritis (Wimberger sign — bilateral metaphyseal destruction), skin rashes (vesiculobullous eruption). Late congenital syphilis (>2 years): Hutchinson''s triad — interstitial keratitis, notched (Hutchinson) incisors, eighth nerve deafness; saddle nose deformity, sabre shins, Clutton joints."]','A','Hutchinson''s triad (interstitial keratitis + notched incisors + 8th nerve deafness) is the pathognomonic late manifestation of congenital syphilis. Antenatal screening (RPR/VDRL at booking, 28 weeks, delivery in high-prevalence areas) and treatment with benzathine penicillin G prevents congenital syphilis. The global resurgence of syphilis (including congenital) since 2010 is a major public health concern.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Sexually Transmitted Infections — Pathology','hard','flashcard','What is the cellular immunopathology of chlamydial infection and why does it cause tubal damage?','["C. trachomatis is an obligate intracellular bacterium that infects columnar epithelial cells of the endocervix, endometrium, and fallopian tube. Intracellular lifecycle: elementary body (infectious, extracellular) → reticulate body (replicating, intracellular) → elementary bodies released by cell lysis. Host immune response: CD4+ Th1 response (IFN-gamma, IL-12); IFN-gamma induces IDO (indoleamine dioxygenase) which depletes tryptophan — C. trachomatis has tryptophan-dependent variant strains that induce persistent infection when tryptophan depleted. Tubal damage: repeated re-infection triggers exaggerated CD4+ Th1 and Th2 responses; heat shock protein 60 (CHSP60) molecular mimicry with human HSP60 drives autoimmune tubal damage and fibrosis independent of active infection."]','A','The molecular mimicry theory explains why women with chlamydial antibodies (evidence of prior infection) have more severe tubal damage than expected from acute inflammation alone. Anti-CHSP60 antibodies cross-react with human HSP60 on tubal epithelium, triggering immune-mediated destruction. This has implications for vaccine development — ideal chlamydial vaccine should avoid CHSP60 antigens.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Sexually Transmitted Infections — Pathology','easy','flashcard','What are the clinical features and pathology of Trichomonas vaginalis infection?','["Trichomonas vaginalis: flagellated protozoan causing the most common non-viral STI worldwide. Pathology: inflammation of vaginal epithelium (colpitis); epithelial cells show cytopathic changes — nuclear enlargement, perinuclear halo (may mimic koilocytes); strawberry cervix (colposcopic finding — punctate haemorrhages); discharge is frothy, yellow-green, malodorous (pH >4.5). Wet mount microscopy: motile pear-shaped flagellated protozoa. PCR/NAAT: highest sensitivity. Asymptomatic in 50% of women; men largely asymptomatic carriers. Complications: increased HIV susceptibility, preterm labour, low birth weight. Treatment: metronidazole (both partners)."]','A','Trichomonas is the only common STI caused by a protozoan. Its cytopathic effects on Pap smear (nuclear changes mimicking LSIL) must not be mistaken for HPV changes — koilocytes have perinuclear halos with nuclear HYPERCHROMASIA and IRREGULAR nuclear membranes, while Trichomonas-associated changes have smoother nuclear membranes. PCR testing is 3x more sensitive than wet mount for diagnosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');

-- Subtopic 11: Infertility — Pathology Causes
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Infertility — Pathology Causes','easy','flashcard','What are the main categories of female infertility and their relative frequencies?','["Female infertility causes: Ovulatory dysfunction (25-30%): PCOS (most common), hyperprolactinaemia, hypothalamic amenorrhoea, premature ovarian insufficiency, thyroid disease, hyperpituitarism. Tubal/peritoneal factor (30-35%): PID/salpingitis (Chlamydia, gonorrhoea), endometriosis, previous ectopic/surgery, congenital (Mullerian anomaly). Uterine factor (10-15%): submucosal fibroids, intrauterine adhesions (Asherman), polyps, congenital uterine anomalies (septate, bicornuate). Cervical factor (5%): mucus hostility, cervical stenosis post-LEEP/conisation. Unexplained infertility (25-30%): all investigations normal but pregnancy not achieved."]','A','The investigation of infertility follows a systematic protocol: (1) Semen analysis (male factor — 30-40% of couples); (2) Ovarian reserve testing (AMH, AFC, Day 2/3 FSH/LH); (3) Mid-luteal progesterone (confirm ovulation); (4) Tubal patency (HSG or laparoscopy + dye); (5) Uterine cavity assessment (SIS, hysteroscopy). Both partners must be investigated simultaneously — mixed cause is common.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Infertility — Pathology Causes','easy','flashcard','What are the WHO semen analysis reference ranges and the terminology for abnormal parameters?','["WHO 2021 lower reference limits (5th percentile): Volume ≥1.4 mL; Total sperm count ≥39 million; Concentration ≥16 million/mL; Total motility (PR+NP) ≥42%; Progressive motility (PR) ≥30%; Vitality ≥54%; Normal morphology ≥4% (Kruger strict). Terminology: Azoospermia — no sperm. Oligospermia — count <16 million/mL. Asthenospermia — reduced motility. Teratospermia — <4% normal forms. Oligoasthenoteratospermia (OAT) — all three abnormal. Leucocytospermia — >1 million WBC/mL (suggests infection/inflammation)."]','A','The WHO 5th edition (2021) updated reference values based on fertile men from 12 countries. Morphology assessment uses Kruger strict criteria (only perfectly normal-appearing sperm counted as normal) — values <4% indicate teratospermia and predict reduced IVF fertilisation rates. Despite low morphology, many men with teratospermia achieve IVF/ICSI success. Repeated semen analysis (2 samples, 3 months apart) is recommended due to within-individual variation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Infertility — Pathology Causes','easy','flashcard','What is ovarian reserve and how is it clinically assessed?','["Ovarian reserve: the quantity (and quality) of the remaining primordial follicle pool. Declines progressively with age — women are born with ~1-2 million primordial follicles; at menarche ~300,000; at menopause <1,000. Assessment: (1) AMH (anti-Mullerian hormone): secreted by granulosa cells of small antral follicles; does not vary significantly with menstrual cycle day; best static marker of ovarian reserve; low AMH = diminished reserve. (2) AFC (antral follicle count): TVUS count of 2-10 mm follicles in both ovaries on days 2-5; correlates directly with response to gonadotrophins. (3) Day 3 FSH: elevated (>10-12 IU/L) indicates diminished reserve (pituitary tries harder to recruit follicles). (4) Day 3 oestradiol: elevated suppresses FSH, masking elevated FSH values."]','A','AMH is the most robust single marker of ovarian reserve — it predicts ovarian response to FSH stimulation in IVF (poor response: AMH <1 ng/mL or AFC <5; high response: AMH >3.5 ng/mL or AFC >20). It is also a diagnostic marker for PCOS (elevated AMH >5.5 ng/mL). AMH does not predict natural fertility directly — women with very low AMH can still conceive naturally but have reduced IVF success.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Infertility — Pathology Causes','medium','flashcard','Describe the pathological mechanism by which endometriosis causes infertility.','["Endometriosis impairs fertility through multiple mechanisms: (1) Tubo-ovarian adhesions: peritubal and periovarian adhesions (from chronic inflammation/scarring) physically block ovum pickup by fimbria. (2) Ovarian endometriomas (chocolate cysts): reduce ovarian reserve by destroying normal cortex; surgical removal further reduces AFC. (3) Altered peritoneal environment: peritoneal fluid contains elevated prostaglandins, cytokines, activated macrophages, and proteases that impair sperm function, oocyte quality, and fertilisation. (4) Impaired endometrial receptivity: altered expression of endometrial adhesion molecules (integrins, HOXA10), ER/PR signalling, and progesterone resistance reduce implantation rates. (5) Psychological: pain and chronic illness."]','A','Endometriosis-associated infertility affects ~50% of women with the condition. The ESHRE guidelines recommend: for Stage I-II disease — expectant management (most effective in young women), ovulation induction with IUI (modestly improves rates), or IVF. For Stage III-IV — IVF is first-line. Surgical treatment of endometriomas before IVF is controversial — improves ovum retrieval access but reduces AFC; a maximum of one endometrioma surgery is recommended before IVF to avoid further compromising ovarian reserve.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Infertility — Pathology Causes','medium','flashcard','What are the causes of male infertility classified by the hypothalamic-pituitary-testicular axis?','["Pre-testicular (hypogonadotrophic hypogonadism — low FSH, LH, testosterone): Kallmann syndrome (GnRH deficiency + anosmia); hyperprolactinaemia; hypothalamic/pituitary tumours; exogenous androgens/anabolic steroids (suppress GnRH/LH — testicular atrophy). Testicular (hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism — elevated FSH, LH; low testosterone): Klinefelter syndrome (47,XXY); Y chromosome microdeletions (AZFa/b/c); cryptorchidism; orchitis (mumps); testicular torsion; chemotherapy/radiation; varicocele. Post-testicular (obstructive azoospermia — normal FSH, LH, testosterone): CBAVD (cystic fibrosis); epididymal obstruction (post-infection); vasectomy; ejaculatory duct obstruction."]','A','The distinction between obstructive azoospermia (OA) and non-obstructive azoospermia (NOA) is critical: OA has normal spermatogenesis (normal FSH, normal testicular volume) but mechanical block — sperm retrieval (PESA/MESA) for ICSI is highly successful. NOA has impaired spermatogenesis (elevated FSH, small testes) — conventional TESE may find rare sperm in ~50% of cases (micro-TESE improves yield). In OA from CBAVD, CFTR mutation testing of both partners is essential before ICSI (risk of CF offspring if partner is a carrier).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Infertility — Pathology Causes','medium','flashcard','What is varicocele, how is it graded, and what is its effect on semen parameters?','["Varicocele: pathological dilation of pampiniform plexus veins in the spermatic cord. Predominantly left-sided (90%) — left testicular vein drains at right angle into left renal vein (vs right testicular vein draining obliquely into IVC). Grades: Grade 1 — palpable only on Valsalva; Grade 2 — palpable without Valsalva; Grade 3 — visible through scrotum. Ultrasound: vein diameter >3 mm on Valsalva (most reliable). Effect on semen: elevated scrotal temperature impairs spermatogenesis → oligospermia, asthenospermia, teratospermia (OAT pattern) with stress pattern morphology (tapered forms). Also reduces Leydig cell testosterone production."]','A','Varicocele is present in 15% of all men but 40% of men investigated for infertility — suggesting causative role. Varicocelectomy (microsurgical, laparoscopic, or percutaneous embolisation) improves semen parameters in most cases and increases natural conception and IVF success rates. However, benefit is most clear in men with clinical varicocele + abnormal semen + primary infertility; evidence for subclinical varicocele treatment is weaker.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Infertility — Pathology Causes','hard','flashcard','What are the uterine Mullerian anomalies and how do they affect fertility?','["Classification (ESHRE/ESGE 2013): Class U0 — Normal. U1 — Dysmorphic (T-shaped — diethylstilboestrol [DES] exposure in utero). U2 — Septate (incomplete midline septum resorption — most common anomaly, 35%; avascular fibromuscular septum; highest miscarriage rate — 65%; correctable by hysteroscopic septoplasty). U3 — Bicorporeal (failure of Mullerian duct fusion — bicornuate; higher preterm birth risk). U4 — Hemi (one Mullerian duct fails to develop — unicornuate; 40% miscarriage rate, 10-15% ectopic risk). U5 — Aplastic (absent uterus — MRKH syndrome: Mayer-Rokitansky-Kuster-Hauser — XX female with absent uterus and proximal vagina). U6 — Unclassified."]','A','Septate uterus (U2) is the most clinically important anomaly — the fibromuscular septum is avascular and cannot support placentation, causing recurrent miscarriage. It is the ONLY congenital uterine anomaly that is amenable to surgical correction with documented improvement in reproductive outcomes (hysteroscopic metroplasty reduces miscarriage rate from ~65% to ~15%). MRKH syndrome requires uterine transplantation (experimental) or surrogacy for genetic parenthood.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Infertility — Pathology Causes','hard','flashcard','What is premature ovarian insufficiency (POI) and what are its causes and management?','["POI (previously premature ovarian failure): hypergonadotrophic hypogonadism in a woman <40 with: (1) menstrual disturbance ≥4 months and (2) elevated FSH >25 IU/L on two measurements ≥4 weeks apart. Causes: Idiopathic (50-70%). Genetic: Turner syndrome/mosaic (45,X/46,XX); FMR1 premutation (fragile X carrier — 20% develop POI); BRCA2 gene (increased risk of early menopause). Autoimmune: adrenal autoantibodies (Addison disease association), thyroid, others; lymphocytic oophoritis on biopsy. Iatrogenic: chemotherapy (especially alkylating agents, cyclophosphamide), pelvic radiotherapy, bilateral oophorectomy. Management: HRT (until natural menopause age ~51) for menopausal symptoms and bone/cardiovascular protection; donor oocyte IVF for fertility."]','A','FMR1 premutation (55-200 CGG repeats) — intermediate between normal (<55) and full mutation (>200, causing Fragile X syndrome) — is associated with POI in 20% of female carriers. Full mutation causes X-linked intellectual disability in sons. All women diagnosed with POI <40 should have FMR1 testing as this has implications for family members (sons risk of FXS; daughters risk of POI). Spontaneous pregnancy occurs in ~5% of women with POI.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Infertility — Pathology Causes','hard','flashcard','What is the pathological basis of PCOS-related anovulation?','["PCOS anovulation pathogenesis: (1) Insulin resistance → compensatory hyperinsulinaemia → stimulates ovarian theca cells (via IGF-1 receptors on theca) → excess androgen synthesis (testosterone, androstenedione) → high intraovarian androgens inhibit normal follicular maturation → follicles arrest at preantral stage (~5-8 mm). (2) LH hypersecretion: elevated LH:FSH ratio (>2:1) due to altered GnRH pulse frequency → excessive theca cell stimulation → further androgen excess → follicular atresia. (3) Excess intraovarian androgens inhibit FSH-driven granulosa cell aromatase (enzyme converting androgens to oestrogen) → arrested follicular development → multiple stalled follicles (subcortical cysts) without ovulation."]','A','The arrested follicles in PCOS are not truly cystic (no pathological fluid) — they are follicles stalled at the antral stage by androgen excess and deficient FSH stimulation. AMH is significantly elevated in PCOS because the large cohort of small antral follicles produces AMH, and AMH itself further inhibits FSH action (negative feedback), creating a self-perpetuating anovulatory cycle. Insulin sensitisers (metformin) reduce hyperinsulinaemia and partially restore ovulation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Infertility — Pathology Causes','easy','flashcard','What is hyperprolactinaemia and how does it cause infertility?','["Hyperprolactinaemia (elevated prolactin >500 mIU/L): most common pituitary endocrine disorder. Causes: Physiological (pregnancy, breastfeeding, stress, sleep). Pharmacological (dopamine antagonists — antipsychotics, metoclopramide, domperidone, methyldopa; oestrogen). Pathological — prolactinoma (pituitary adenoma — most common cause of pathological hyperprolactinaemia); hypothyroidism (TRH stimulates prolactin); renal failure; macroprolactinaemia (large prolactin complexes — often not clinically significant). Mechanism of infertility: elevated prolactin inhibits GnRH pulsatility → reduced LH/FSH → anovulation, oligomenorrhoea/amenorrhoea. Also causes galactorrhoea (in ~30%). Treatment: dopamine agonists (cabergoline, bromocriptine) — suppress prolactin and reduce tumour size."]','A','Prolactin is under tonic inhibitory control by dopamine from the hypothalamus. Any cause that disrupts dopaminergic inhibition (drugs, stalk compression by pituitary tumours) elevates prolactin. Cabergoline (long-acting D2 receptor agonist) is highly effective — normalises prolactin and restores ovulation in >90% of cases. Pituitary MRI should be performed to exclude macroadenoma (>10 mm) vs microadenoma (<10 mm) in all cases of pathological hyperprolactinaemia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');

-- Additional flashcards to reach 10 per subtopic
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES

-- Subtopic 4 extra
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Uterine Pathology','easy','flashcard','What are the symptoms of uterine leiomyomas and how does their position influence symptoms?','["Symptoms depend on location: Submucosal fibroids (inside cavity): menorrhagia (most significant), dysmenorrhoea, infertility (distort endometrial cavity, impair implantation), recurrent miscarriage — even small submucosal fibroids can be symptomatic. Intramural fibroids (within myometrium): menorrhagia, pelvic pressure/heaviness, bulk symptoms (urinary frequency, constipation). Subserosal fibroids (projecting outward): bulk symptoms, urinary frequency, rarely constipation; pedunculated subserosal may tort. Cervical/broad ligament fibroids: bladder/bowel pressure. Pedunculated submucosal (''polyp'') may prolapse through cervical os."]','A','The mnemonic PALM-COEIN (Polyp, Adenomyosis, Leiomyoma, Malignancy — Coagulopathy, Ovulatory dysfunction, Endometrial, Iatrogenic, Not yet classified) is the FIGO classification system for abnormal uterine bleeding. Leiomyomas are classified by FIGO subtype 0-8 based on location (0 = pedunculated intracavitary, 1 = submucosal <50% intramural, 2 = submucosal ≥50% intramural, 3-5 = intramural/transmural, 6-7 = subserosal, 8 = cervical/other).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

-- Subtopic 5 extra
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Gestational Trophoblastic Disease','medium','flashcard','What is the most common non-molar antecedent pregnancy for gestational choriocarcinoma?','["Choriocarcinoma can follow ANY type of pregnancy: Hydatidiform mole — 50% (most common antecedent, highest per-pregnancy risk ~1:40 complete moles). Normal term pregnancy — 25% (most common in absolute numbers due to high frequency of normal pregnancies; per-pregnancy risk very low ~1:50,000). Ectopic pregnancy — rare (~2%). Spontaneous abortion — ~25%. Key point: choriocarcinoma can occur MONTHS TO YEARS after any pregnancy. All women with persistent uterine bleeding after any pregnancy should have serum hCG measured. Choriocarcinoma following term pregnancy is often misdiagnosed as postpartum haemorrhage or retained products."]','A','Because term pregnancies are so much more common than molar pregnancies, the absolute number of choriocarcinomas arising from term pregnancies rivals those from molar pregnancies, even though the risk per term pregnancy is 1000x lower. This is why ALL women with suspected GTN should have a thorough reproductive history taken to establish the antecedent pregnancy type, as this affects the FIGO risk score and prognosis.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

-- Subtopic 6 extra
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Breast Disease','medium','flashcard','What is the sentinel lymph node biopsy (SLNB) concept in breast cancer surgery?','["Sentinel lymph node (SLN) = the first lymph node(s) receiving lymphatic drainage from the primary tumour. If the SLN is negative for metastasis, it is highly unlikely that other axillary nodes are involved (negative predictive value >97%). Technique: intradermal/peritumoral injection of blue dye (patent blue) ± radiolabelled colloid (technetium-99m) — SLN identified by blue colour and/or radioactivity on gamma probe. SLN is examined intraoperatively (frozen section or imprint cytology) and sent for detailed histological assessment (H&E + cytokeratin IHC). If SLN negative: no further axillary surgery needed. If SLN positive: completion axillary lymph node dissection (ALND) or axillary radiotherapy — reduces morbidity vs mandatory ALND."]','A','SLNB has replaced routine ALND for clinically node-negative breast cancer, dramatically reducing lymphoedema rates (from 15-20% with ALND to <5% with SLNB). The ACOSOG Z0011 trial showed that in women with 1-2 positive SLNs undergoing breast-conserving surgery + whole breast radiotherapy + systemic therapy, completion ALND does not improve survival, allowing many node-positive women to avoid ALND.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

-- Subtopic 7 extra
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Early','hard','flashcard','What is cervical incompetence and what are its pathological features?','["Cervical incompetence (cervical insufficiency): painless cervical dilation and pregnancy loss in the second trimester (typically 14-24 weeks) without uterine contractions or labour. Pathological features: deficiency of cervical connective tissue (collagen, proteoglycans); abnormally high smooth muscle content; failure of normal cervical ''ripening'' regulation. Causes: structural (congenital — Mullerian anomalies, DES exposure causing T-shaped uterus/cervical hypoplasia); acquired (previous cervical trauma — LEEP/cone biopsy removing significant cervical stroma, forcible dilation, obstetric lacerations). Diagnosis: clinical history of second-trimester loss + ultrasound finding of short cervix (<25 mm on TVUS at 16-24 weeks). Management: McDonald cerclage (suture placed around cervix at 12-14 weeks); arabin pessary; progesterone (natural progesterone vaginal pessaries reduce preterm birth risk in short cervix)."]','A','The distinction between cervical incompetence and preterm labour is clinically important: incompetence = painless dilation, no contractions; preterm labour = painful contractions with dilation. In practice, they overlap on a spectrum. Cervical length measurement by TVUS at 20 weeks is the best predictor of preterm birth risk in asymptomatic women (length <25 mm → high risk; <15 mm → very high risk). Progestogen supplementation reduces preterm birth risk by ~45% in women with short cervix.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

-- Subtopic 8 extra
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Pregnancy Complications — Late','medium','flashcard','What is obstetric cholestasis (intrahepatic cholestasis of pregnancy) and its key pathological concern?','["Obstetric cholestasis (ICP): reversible, pregnancy-specific cholestasis occurring in the third trimester. Symptoms: generalised pruritus (worst on palms and soles, worse at night) without rash; may have jaundice (10-20%). Labs: elevated bile acids (serum total bile acids ≥10 μmol/L — diagnostic); elevated ALT/AST; elevated bilirubin in severe cases; normal GGT. Pathological concern: elevated fetal bile acids cause cardiac arrhythmias in the fetus → unexplained intrauterine fetal death (IUFD) risk increases, particularly with total bile acids ≥100 μmol/L. Management: ursodeoxycholic acid (UDCA — reduces bile acids and pruritus); increased fetal surveillance; delivery at 37-38 weeks (or earlier if bile acids ≥100 μmol/L) to prevent IUFD."]','A','ICP recurs in 45-90% of subsequent pregnancies and with combined oral contraceptive use. It is associated with heterozygous mutations in hepatocanalicular transporters (ABCB4 — MDR3, ABCB11 — BSEP, ABCC2 — MRP2). The mechanism of fetal cardiac arrhythmia: elevated fetal taurocholate inhibits L-type calcium channels in fetal cardiomyocytes, causing conduction abnormalities and sudden cardiac death. UDCA improves maternal symptoms but evidence for improving fetal outcomes is less certain.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

-- Subtopic 9 extra
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Male Reproductive Pathology','medium','flashcard','What is testicular torsion and what are the pathological consequences?','["Testicular torsion: rotation of the testis on the spermatic cord, twisting the testicular vessels → venous obstruction → oedema → arterial compromise → testicular infarction. Most common in adolescent males (peak 12-18 years). Types: intravaginal torsion (most common — high insertion of tunica vaginalis: ''bell-clapper deformity'' predisposes); extravaginal torsion (neonates — before fixation). Time to irreversible ischaemia: 6 hours → 90-100% salvage; 12 hours → 50% salvage; 24 hours → 10% salvage; >24 hours → essentially zero. Pathology: haemorrhagic infarction of testis and epididymis; venous engorgement. Presentation: sudden severe scrotal pain, nausea/vomiting, absent cremasteric reflex, high-riding transverse testis. Management: immediate surgical exploration (no time for investigations if clinical suspicion high) — manual detorsion followed by orchidopexy (bilateral — ''bell-clapper'' is bilateral in 80%)."]','A','The cremasteric reflex (stroking inner thigh → ipsilateral testicular elevation) is absent in testicular torsion due to spermatic cord rotation preventing the cremasteric muscle from contracting. Its absence is the single most sensitive clinical sign (99% sensitive). Colour Doppler ultrasound (absent intratesticular flow) confirms diagnosis but MUST NOT delay surgery — time is testis. If there is any clinical doubt, explore surgically.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed'),

-- Subtopic 10 extra
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Reproductive System & OB-GYN','Pathology','Sexually Transmitted Infections — Pathology','medium','flashcard','What is the pathogenesis and clinical presentation of bacterial vaginosis (BV)?','["BV: most common vaginal condition in reproductive-age women (not classically an STI but sexually associated). Pathogenesis: disruption of normal Lactobacillus-dominant vaginal flora → overgrowth of anaerobes (Gardnerella vaginalis, Mobiluncus, Prevotella, Mycoplasma hominis). Normal vaginal pH: 3.8-4.2 (lactic acid from Lactobacillus). BV pH: >4.5 (reduced lactic acid, increased amines from anaerobes). Clinical: thin, homogeneous grey-white vaginal discharge; fishy odour (amines — putrescine, cadaverine — especially on addition of 10% KOH — positive ''whiff test''). Diagnosis — Amsel criteria (3 of 4): homogeneous discharge; pH >4.5; positive whiff test; clue cells (vaginal epithelial cells coated with bacteria on wet mount — stippled, indistinct cell borders). Gram stain (Nugent score) is gold standard. Treatment: metronidazole or clindamycin."]','A','BV increases susceptibility to HIV and other STIs (disruption of mucosal immune barrier) and causes obstetric complications: preterm birth, PPROM, post-partum endometritis. Recurrence is common (50% within 12 months) because treatment restores Lactobacillus temporarily. Attempts to restore healthy vaginal microbiome using vaginal probiotics (Lactobacillus reuteri or rhamnosus) show promise in reducing recurrence. BV is not a reportable disease and male treatment does not reduce recurrence.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Robbins & Cotran Pathologic Basis of Disease 9th Ed');
